1100:
** γλῶσσα**,
_Ionic dialect_ **γλάσσα**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 3.84, +others, “SIG” 1002.7 (Milet.), “Schwyzer” 692 (from Chios)}}, _Attic dialect_ **γλῶττα**, ης, ἡ, **tongue**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.332, etc.}}
**b** γ. λάρυγγος, ={γλωττίς}, **larynx**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “de Usu Partium” 7.13.}}
**2** **tongue**, as the organ of speech, γλώσσης χάριν through love **of talking**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 709, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 266; γλώσσῃ ματαίᾳ [prev. author] “Pr.” 331, compare “Eu.” 830; γλώσσης ἀκρατής [prev. author] “Pr.” 884 (Lyric poetry); μεγάλης γ. κόμποι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 128}}; γλώσσῃ δεινός, θρασύς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “OC” 806, “Aj.” 1142; ἡ γ. ὀμώμοχ᾽ ἡ δὲ φρὴν ἀνώμοτος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 612: with Preps., ἀπὸ γλώσσης}} by **frankness of speech**, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 63; φθέγγεσθαι 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 6.13 (but ἀπὸ γ. ληίσσεται, opposed to χερσὶ βίῃ}}, of fraud opposed to violence, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 322)}}; also, by **word of mouth**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.123, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.10, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 2.14.1; τῷ νῷ θ᾽ ὁμοίως κἀπὸ τῆς γ. λέγω 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 936}}; τὰ γλώσσης ἄπο, i.e. **our words**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 1049}}; ἀπὸ γ. φράσω by **heart**, opposed to γράμμασιν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 122}}; οὐκ ἀπὸ γλώσσης not from **mere word of mouth**, but after full argument, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 813}}; μὴ διὰ γλώσσης without using **the tongue**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 112; ἐν ὄμμασιν.. δεδορκὼς κοὐ κατὰ γλῶσσαν κλύων 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 747:}} —phrases: πᾶσαν γλῶτταν βασάνιζε try every **art of tongue**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 547}}; πᾶσαν ἱέναι γλῶσσαν let loose one's whole **tongue**, speak withoutrestraint, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 596; πολλὴν γ. ἐγχέας μάτην [prev. author] “fragment” 929}}; κακὰ γ. slander, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.283: _plural_, ἐν κερτομίοις γλώσσαις}}, i.e. with blasphemies, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 962 (Lyric poetry), compare “Aj.” 199 (Lyric poetry): βοῦς, κ ῇς ἐπὶ γλώσσῃ, see at {βοῦς, κλείς}.}}]
**3** of persons, **one who is all tongue, speaker**, of Pericles, μεγίστη γ. τῶν Ἑλληνίδων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 293, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 629 (assuming variant).}}
**4** ἡ γ. τοῦ ταμιείου the **advocacy** of the fiscus, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vitae Sophistarum” 2.29.}}
**II** **language**, ἄλλη δ᾽ ἄλλων γ. μεμιγμένη {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.175, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.804}}; γλῶσσαν ἱέναι speak a **language** or **dialect**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.57}}; γ. Ἑλληνίδα, Δωρίδα ἱέναι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 9.16, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.112, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 406, “Ch.” 564; γλῶσσαν νομίζειν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.142, 4.183; γλώσσῃ χρῆσθαι [prev. author] 4.109; κατὰ τὴν ἀρχαίαν γ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1357b10}}; **dialect**, ἡ Ἀττικὴ γ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 177; but also Δωρὶς διάλεκτος μία ὑφ᾽ ἥν εἰσι γ. πολλαί 3rd-4th c.AD: Tryphiodorus Epicus cited in Scholia 2nd c.BC: Dionysius Thrax Grammaticus p.320 H.}}
**II.2** **obsolete** or **foreign word**, which needs explanation, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1410b12, “Po.” 1457b4, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.406f: hence Γλῶσσαι, title of works by Philemon and others.}}
**II.3** **people speaking a distinct language**,{{LXX+4th c.BC+|LXX.Jdt.3.8 (_plural_), interpolated in 4th c.BC(?): Scylax Geographus 15.}}
**III** **anything shaped like the tongue** {{NT|compare (γλῶσσαι ὡσεὶ πυρός NT.Act.2.3).}}
**III.1** in Music, **rced** or **tongue of a pipe**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.229, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 565a24, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 4.11.4, etc.}}
**III.2** **tongue** or **thong of leather, shoe-latchet**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Comicus 51, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Socraticus Philosophus 57.}}
**III.3** **tongue of land**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Αιβυκή” 121, compare 95.}}
**III.4** **ingot**, γ. χρυσῆ {{LXX|LXX.Josh.7.21.}}
**III.5** **marking** on the liver, in divination, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus (γλῶσσα}} from γλωχ-**y[acaron]**, compare γλώξ, γλωχί; γλάσσα from *γλᾰχ-**y[acaron]**, weak grade of same root.)

1101:
Related to: ** γλωσσό-κομος**, ὁ,
**sarcophagus**, {{Refs|“BSA” 17.235 (from Pamphylia).}}
**II** **surgical instrument**, used for reducing fractures and dislocations, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Heliodorus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 49.20.}}
**II.b** **box-splint**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 10.442, “UP” 7.14.}}
**III** **pudenda muliebria**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 142.}}

1102:
** κνᾰφ-εύς**, έως, ὁ,
**fuller, cloth-carder** or **dresser,** {{Refs 6th c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 12.436 (6th c.BC), 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.14, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1128, “Ec.” 415: as _feminine_, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 760}}; **γνᾰφ-**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 1.21, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 3.16 codices, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 1.26}}, and usually in later Gr., {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 4.78, “PCair.Zen.” 206.48 (3rd c.BC), “IG” 12(2).271 (from Mytilene), “POxy.” 736.37 (1st c.AD)}}, etc.; but κναφεύς {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PIand.” 43.13 (2nd c.AD).}}
**II** γναφεύς, kind of **fish**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dorio Rerum Naturalium Scriptor cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 7.297c.}}

1103:
** γνήσι-ος**, α, ον,
(γένος) **belonging to the race**, i. e. **lawfully begotten, born in wedlock**, νόθον καὶ γνήσιον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.102, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.202, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.2, “Leg.Gort.” 10.41, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1665, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.127, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 44.49, etc.; παίδων ἐπ᾽ ἀρότῳ γνησίων 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Περικειρομένη” 435; ἀδελφός 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1659; νόθος.. γνησίοις ἴσως σθένει 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 87; φρονοῦντα γνήσια 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 309; γ. φρόνημα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 307.}}
**2** generally, **genuine, legitimate**, φίλος {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Phocylides Lyricus 2}} A; γ. γυναῖκες **lawful** wives, opposed to παλλακίδες, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.3.1; πολῖται 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1278a30, compare 1319b9}}; γ. τῆς Ἑλλάδος **true** Greeks, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 9.30; ἀκουστής 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Isocrate” 18 (_superlative_)}}; μήτηρ τῶν ἐρωτικῶν λόγων, of Aphrodite, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Am.” 19}}; γ. ἀρεταί **real, unfeigned** virtues, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 2.11}}; γ. ὕμνοι **inspired** song, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 8.83}}; of fevers, γ. τριταῖος a **genuine** tertian, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προγνωστικόν” 24}}; γ. ὄξος **genuine** vinegar, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 65}}; of writings, **genuine**, [{{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.748, 1st-2nd c.AD: Harpocratio Grammaticus see at {Ἀλκιβιάδης}.}}] _adverb_ -ίως **genuinely, truly**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 678, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 2.76, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator “Epistulae” 3.32, etc.}}; γ. φέρειν bear **nobly**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 281, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 205}}; **lawfully**, τοῖς γ. συμβιώσασιν {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “de Pietate - Herkulanische Studien” 93.}}
**II** γνήσια, τά, **charges on land**, γ. δημόσια {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“PAmh.” 86.15 (1st c.AD),compare “PLond.” 3.1157.4 (2nd c.AD).}}

1104:
Included with: ** γνήσι-ος**, α, ον,
