**2** generally, **genuine, legitimate**, φίλος {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Phocylides Lyricus 2}} A; γ. γυναῖκες **lawful** wives, opposed to παλλακίδες, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.3.1; πολῖται 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1278a30, compare 1319b9}}; γ. τῆς Ἑλλάδος **true** Greeks, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 9.30; ἀκουστής 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Isocrate” 18 (_superlative_)}}; μήτηρ τῶν ἐρωτικῶν λόγων, of Aphrodite, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Am.” 19}}; γ. ἀρεταί **real, unfeigned** virtues, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 2.11}}; γ. ὕμνοι **inspired** song, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 8.83}}; of fevers, γ. τριταῖος a **genuine** tertian, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προγνωστικόν” 24}}; γ. ὄξος **genuine** vinegar, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 65}}; of writings, **genuine**, [{{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.748, 1st-2nd c.AD: Harpocratio Grammaticus see at {Ἀλκιβιάδης}.}}] _adverb_ -ίως **genuinely, truly**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 678, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 2.76, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator “Epistulae” 3.32, etc.}}; γ. φέρειν bear **nobly**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 281, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 205}}; **lawfully**, τοῖς γ. συμβιώσασιν {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “de Pietate - Herkulanische Studien” 93.}}
**II** γνήσια, τά, **charges on land**, γ. δημόσια {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“PAmh.” 86.15 (1st c.AD),compare “PLond.” 3.1157.4 (2nd c.AD).}}

1106:
** γνώμ-η**, ἡ,
**means of knowing**: hence, **mark, token**, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 60 (_plural_)}}; of the teeth {{Refs 4th c.BC+|compare (γνώμων 111), 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 576b15.}}
**II** **organ by which one perceives** or **knows, intelligence**,
**II.1** **thought, judgement** (τῆς ψυχῆς ἡ γ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 672b), ἐκμαθεῖν ψυχήν τε καὶ φρόνημα καὶ γ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 176: _accusative_ _absolutely_, γνώμην ἱκανός}} **intelligent**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.4}}; γ. ἀγαθός, κακός, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 687, “Ph.” 910; τοιάδε τὴν γ. [prev. author] “El.” 1021; κατὰ γ. ἴδρις [prev. author] “OT” 1087 (Lyric poetry); γνώμᾳ διπλόαν θέτο βουλάν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 10.89; γνώμῃ μαθεῖν τι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 403; γνώμῃ κυρήσας [prev. author] “OT” 398}}; γνώμῃ φρενῶν, opposed to ὀργῇ,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 524; γνώμης ξύνεσις 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.75; γνώμης μᾶλλον ἐφόδῳ ἢ ἰσχύος [prev. author] 3.11; ταῖς γ. καὶ τοῖς σώμασι σφάλλεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.3.10, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.70}}; γνώμῃ, opposed to τύχῃ, σωφρονοῦντες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 3.47}}; γνώμης ἅπτεσθαι affect **the head**, of wine or fever, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης ὀξέων” 63, “Fract.” 11}}; γνώμην ἔχειν **understand**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 214 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 396; πάντων γ. ἴσχειν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 837 (Lyric poetry)}}; προσέχειν γνώμην give **heed, attend**, δεῦρο τὴν γ. προσίσχετε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 37; πρὸς ἕτερον γνώμην ἔχειν 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.192}}; to be on one's **guard**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.95}}; δηλοῦν τὴν γ. ἔν τινι to show one's **wit** in.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] 3.37; ἐν γνώμῃ τι παραστῆσαι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.17}}; ἀπὸ γνώμης φέρειν ψῆφον δικαίαν with a good **conscience**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 674}}; but οὐκ ἀπὸ γ. λέγεις not without **judgement**, with good **sense**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 389; ἄτερ γνώμης 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 456; ἄνευ γ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 594}}; γνώμῃ κολάζειν **with good reason**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.6.10}}; γνώμῃ τῇ ἀρίστῃ (i.e. κρίνειν or δικάζειν) **to** the best of **one's judgement**, in the dicasts' oath, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1375a29; ἡ καλουμένη γ. τοῦ ἐπιεικοῦς κρίσις ὀρθή [prev. author] “EN” 1143a19; so περὶ ὧν ἂν νόμοι μὴ ὦσι, γνώμῃ τῇ δικαιοτάτῃ κρινεῖν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.118; γ. τῇ δ. δικάσειν ὀμωμόκασιν [prev. author] 23.96, compare 39.40; τῇ δ. γ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1287a26}}; ὅστις γνώμῃ μὴ καθαρεύει has not a clear **conscience**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 355.}}
**II.2** **will, disposition, inclination**, εὐσεβεῖ γνώμᾳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 3.41; γ. Διός 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1003}}; ἐν γνώμῃ γεγονέναι τινί to stand **high in his favour**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.37}}; πάσῃ τῇ γ. with all **one's zeal**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.45; τίνα αὐτοὺς οἴεσθε γ. ἕξειν περὶ σφῶν αὐτῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.104; γ. ἔ. περί τινα 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 10.21}}; πρὸς τοὺς Ἀθηναίους τὴν γ. ἔχειν **to be inclined** towards.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.44}}; ἐμπιμπλάναι τὴν γ. τινός satisfy his **wishes**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.7.8, compare “HG” 6.1.15 (_plural_)}}; ἀφ᾽ ἑαυτοῦ γνώμης on his own **initiative**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.68}}; ἐκ μιᾶς γ. of one **accord**, with one **consent**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 10.59; μιᾷ γνώμῃ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.122, 6.17; διὰ μιᾶς γ. γίγνεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.139}}; κατὰ γνώμην according to one's **mind** or **wishes**, ὅταν τἀκεῖ θῶ κατὰ γνώμην ἐμήν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 737; ἄν τι μὴ κατὰ γ. ἐκβῇ 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.16: in _plural_, φίλιαι γνῶμαι}} friendly **sentiments**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.4.}}
**III** **judgement, opinion**, βροτῶν γ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Parmenides Poeta Philosophus 8.61}}; ταύτῃ.. τῇ γνώμῃ πλεῖστός εἰμι I in cline mostly **to this view**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.220 (assuming variant); also ταύτῃ πλεῖστος τὴν γνώμην εἰμί [prev. author] 1.120; ἡ πλείστη γ. ἐστί τινι [prev. author] 5.126; τλέον φέρει ἡ γ. τινί [prev. author] 8.100; τὸ πλεῖστον τῆς γ. εἶχεν.. προσμεῖξαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.31; γνώμην τίθεσθαι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.80}}; οὕτως τὴν γ. ἔχειν to be of this **opinion**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.15, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.2.8, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 157; εἴ τινι γ. τοιαύτη παρειστήκει περὶ ἐμοῦ 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.54; τὴν αὐτὴν γ. ἔχειν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.55}}; τῆς αὐτῆς γ. εἶναι, ἔχεσθαι, {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.113, 140; ὁ αὐτὸς εἰμὶ τῇ γ. [prev. author] 3.38}}; κατὰ γ. τὴν ἐμήν in my **judgement** or **opinion**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.26, 5.3; elliptically, κατά γε τὴν ἐμήν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 153, compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 18.1.18, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Isocrate” 3: _absolutely_, γνώμην ἐμήν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 983, “Pax” 232}}; παρὰ γνώμην τοῖς Ἕλλησιν ἐγένετο contrary to **general opinion**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.40}}; but παρὰ γ. κινδυνευταί **reckless** venturers, {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.70, compare 4.19}}; εἰπὲ μὴ παρὰ γ. ἐμοί either contrary to **my wish**, or contrary to **your true opinion**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 931, compare “Supp.” 454}}: frequently of **opinions** delivered publicly, ἑστάναι πρὸς τὴν γ. τινός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.56}}; Θεμιστοκλέους γνώμῃ **by the advice** of {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus, [prev. author] 1.90, 93}}; γνώμην ἀποφαίνειν deliver **an opinion**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.40}}; ἀποδείκνυσθαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 207; ἐκφαίνειν [prev. author] 5.36; τίθεσθαι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1448 (_anapaest meter_), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 658; ἀποφαίνεσθαι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 336; ποιεῖσθαι περί τινων 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.36}}; γνώμας κατέθεντο have made up their **minds**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Parmenides Poeta Philosophus 8.53.}}
**III.b** **verdict**, ἡ τοῦ δικαστοῦ γ. {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“IG” 4.364 (Corinth, 4th c.AD), compare 685.32 (_plural_, Cretan dialect, 2nd c.BC).}}
**III.2** **proposition, motion**, γνώμην εἰσφέρειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.80, 81; εἰπεῖν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.68, etc.}}; (but γνώμας προτιθέναι hold a debate, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.36); γνῶμαι τρεῖς προεκέατο 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.83}}: frequently in Inscrr., **resolution,** {{Refs|Inscriptiones Graecae 12.118.28, etc.; γ. στρατηγῶν [prev. work]22.27; Κλεισόφου καὶ συμπρυτάνεων [prev. work]1; ἡ ἐκφερομένη γ. [prev. work]1051c26; γνώμην νικᾶν}} carry **a motion**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 594, “Nu.” 432; κρατεῖν τῇ γ. 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Coriolanus” 17.}}
**III.3** γνῶμαι, αἱ, **practical maxims**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 78, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1091, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.2.9, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1395a11 (_sing._, 1394a22).}}
**III.4** in _plural_, **fancies, illusions**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 52.}}
**III.5** **intention, purpose, resolve**, ἀπὸ τοιᾶσδε γνώμης with some such **purpose** as this, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.92}}; γνώμην ποιεῖ σθαι, c._infinitive_, **propose** to do, {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.128}}; κατὰ γνώμην of **set purpose**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 6.81 (so also γνώμης 4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Orationes” 33.13, 50.12)}}; τίνα ἔχουσα γνώμη; with what **purpose?** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.119}}; οἶδα δ᾽ οὐ γνώμῃ τίν; with what **intent?** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 527, compare “Aj.” 448}}; ἡ ξύμπασα γ. τῶν λεχθέντων the general **purport**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.22}}; ἦν τοῦ τείχους ἡ γνώμη.., ἵνα.. **the purpose** of it was.., that.., {{Refs|[prev. author] 8.90.}}

1107:
** γνωρ-ίζω**,
_future_ _Attic dialect_-ῐῶ: _perfect_ ἐγνώρικα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 262b}}:—**make known, point out**, {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 487, LXX.1Ki.10.8, +others, NT.Rom.0.22}}:—in this sense mostly _passive_, **become known**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 428a, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Priora” 64b35; τὰ γνωριζόμενα μέρη τῆς οἰκουμένης 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.37.4.}}
**2** with _accusative_ _person_, **make known**, τινά τινι {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Fabius Maximus” 21}}; **commend**, τινὰ τῇ βουλῇ ἰσχυρῶς {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Μακεδονική” 9.6.}}
**3** **certify** a person's **identity**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 581.13 (2nd c.AD), “POxy.” 1024.18 (2nd c.AD).}}
**II** **gain knowledge of, become acquainted with, discover**, with _participle_, τοὔργον ὡς οὐ γνωριοῖμί σου τόδε δόλῳ προσέρπον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 538; τὰ καλὰ γ. οἱ εὐφυέες πρὸς αὐτά 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 56, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 564, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.44, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 184a12}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.103, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 72}}; γ. περί τι or περί τινος {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1005b8, 1037a16.}}
**II.2** **become acquainted with**, τινά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 181c, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 35.6; τινὰς ὁποῖοί τινές εἰσι 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 2.28:}} — _passive_, ἐγνωρισμένοι αὐτῷ **being made acquainted with** him, {{Refs|[prev. passage]}}; πρός τινος {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tim.” 5.}}

1108:
** γνῶσις**, εως, ἡ,
**seeking to know, inquiry, investigation**, especially judicial, τὰς τῶν δικαστηρίων γ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.224; τὴν κατὰ τοῦ διαιτητοῦ γ. [prev. author] 21.92, compare 7.9, 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 141; γ. περὶ τῆς δίκης “PHib.” 1.92.13 (3rd c.BC).}}
**2** **result of investigation, decision,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|PPetr. 3p.118 (3rd c.BC).}}
**II** **knowing, knowledge**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 56}}; opposed to ἀγνωσίη, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης” 1.23 (uncertain)}}; opposed to ἄγνοια, {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 478c; ἡ αἴσθησις γ. τις 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 731a33: _plural_, Θεὸς γνώσεων κύριος LXX.1Ki.2.3.}}
**II.b** **higher, esoteric knowledge**, {{NT|NT.1Cor.8.7, 10, NT.Eph.3.19, etc.; χαρισάμενος ἡμῖν νοῦν, λόγον, γνῶσιν “PMag.Par.” 2.290.}}
**II.2** **acquaintance with** a person, πρός τινα Test. cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.50; τῶν Σεβαστῶν “IPE” 1.47.6 (from Olbia).}}
**II.3** **recognizing**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.44.}}
**II.4** **means of knowing**, [αἱ αἰσθήσεις] κυριώταται τῶν καθ᾽ ἕκαστα γ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 981b11.}}
**III** **being known**, γνῶσιν ἔχει τι, ={γνωστόν ἐστι}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 206b.}}
**III.2** **fame, credit**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 7.5.5, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Herod.” 3.}}
**IV** **means of knowing**: hence, **statement in writing,** {{Refs 6th c.AD+|PLond. 5.1708, etc. (6th c.AD).}}
**V** ={γνῶμα}, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus s. Hymns of see}}

1109:
** γνώστ-ης**, ου, ὁ,
**one that knows**, τῶν ἐθῶν {{NT+3rd c.AD+|NT.Act.26.3; τοῦ εὐαγγελίου “Sammelb.” 421.1 (3rd c.AD)}}: especially**one who knows the future, diviner**, {{LXX|LXX.1Ki.28.3.}}
**II** ={γνωστήρ}, **surety**, γ. τῆς πίστεως {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Flamininus” 4}}; **expert witness** or **valuer,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|PLips. 106.10 (1st c.AD).}}

1110:
** γνωστός**, ή, όν,
collateral form of γνωτός (which see), **known**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 702, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 361, “fragment” 203, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 205d, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.3.44, etc.}}; γνωστόν, τό, **common knowledge**, τινός {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“PAmh.” 145.9 (4th-5th c.AD).}} _adverb_ -τῶς **clearly**, {{LXX|LXX.Pro.27.23, 12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 1540.1.}}
**2** **knowable**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1016b20, “Anthologia Graeca” 0.64b37}}; γνωστὰ σαρκός bodily **symptoms** (of anger), {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” p.24 W.}}
**II** _plural_, as _substantive_, ={γνώριμοι}, **notables**, {{LXX+2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Symmachus LXX.Pro.31.23}}; **acquaintance, friend,** {{NT|NT.Luke.2.44, +others}}
**III** _active_, **knowing**, uncertain in {{LXX+1st c.AD+|LXX.Gen.2.9 (γνωστικός cited in 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.37).}}

1111:
** γογγύζω**,
_Ionic dialect_ {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|(2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista 336) and later Gr. for _Attic dialect_ τονθορύζω}}, **mutter, murmur, grumble**, ἐπί τινι {{LXX+NT+3rd c.BC+|LXX.Num.14.29, compare 17.5; κατά τινος NT.Matt.20.11; περί τινος NT.John.6.41, etc.: _absolutely_, “PPetr.” 3p.130 (3rd c.BC), LXX.Num.11.1, “POxy.” 33 iii 14 (2nd c.AD), 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 1.29.55.}}
**2** of doves, **coo**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 5.89}}. (Cf. Sanskrit _ga[ndot]gūyati_ 'utter cries of joy'.)

1114:
** γόης**, ητος, ὁ,
**sorcerer, wizard**, Phoronis 2, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.33, 4.105, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 380d, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” p.29 W.; γ. ἐπῳδὸς Λυδίας ἀπὸ χθονός 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 234, compare “Hipp.” 1038}}; probably _falsa lectio_ for{βοῇσι} {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.191.}}
**2** **juggler, cheat**, δεινὸς γ. καὶ φαρμακεὺς καὶ σοφιστής {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 203d; δεινὸν καὶ γ. καὶ σοφιστὴν.. ὀνομάζων 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.276; ἄπιστος γ. πονηρός [prev. author] 19.109; μάγος καὶ γ. 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.137}}: _comparative_ γοητότερος {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Achilles Tatius Astronomus 6.7 (assuming variant)}}. (Cf. Lithuanian _žavēti_ 'incantare'.)

1117:
** γόμος**, ὁ,
(γέμω) **ship's freight, cargo**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 444 (uncertain), 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 32.4}}; πεντακισχιλίων ταλάντων γόμον ἔχειν to be of 5,000 talents **burden**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.194.}}
**2** **beast's load**, {{LXX+2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Babrius Fabularum Scriptor 7.11, LXX.Exo.23.5, +others, “PAmh.” 2.138.11 (_plural_, 4th c.AD)}}; γ. καμηλικός {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“OGI” 629.87, +others (Palmyra, 2nd c.AD); γ. καρρικός [prev. work]16.}}
**3** **guild of transport-agents** in Nubia, {{Refs|“CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 4980, +others}}
**II** γὁμος· ζωμός, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}

1119:
** γόνῠ**, τό,
_genitive_ γόνατος, _Epic dialect_ and _Ionic dialect_ γούνατος (for γόνϝατος) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.591, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.80}}: _plural_ _nominative_
**A** γούνατα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.176, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 587, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.199, “Schwyzer” 694.7 (Chios, 4th c.BC), _genitive_ γουνάτων 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.76, _dative_ γούνασι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.455, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.152 (also 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 2.26), γονάτεσσι 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 16.11, “epigram Gr.” 782 (Halic.)}}; also _Epic dialect_ _genitive_ γουνός (explanation as for γόνυος by Hdn.Gr.2.768, [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 342.9) 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.547: _plural_ γοῦνα 6.511; γούνων 1.407, +others: _dative_ γούνεσσι 9.488, +others (variant{γούνασσι})}}]:—_Aeolic dialect_ _accusative_ _plural_ γόνα [{{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 39.7 (probably), but γόννα _accusative_ to 5th c.AD(?): Stephanus Byzantius see entry {Γόννοι}, 12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 335.39: _genitive_ _plural_ γόνων 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 10:}}]
**E** has γουνάτων {{Refs|“Hec.” 752, 839, γούνασι “Supp.” 285 (Lyric poetry), “Andr.” 529 (Lyric poetry), but not γουνός (γοῦν᾽}} _accusative_ _plural_ was read by {{Refs 5th c.AD+|Scholia in “Ph.” 852): _genitive_ _plural_ γεύνων, 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}:—**knee**, γόνυ γουνὸς ἀμείβων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.547, etc.}}: frequently of **clasping the knees** in earnest supplication, ἥψατο γούνων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1.512; ἑλεῖν, λαβεῖν γούνων 21.71, 1.407, etc.; τῶν γουνάτων λαβέσθαι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.76}}; ποτὶ (variant{περὶ}) or ἀμφὶ γούνασί τινος χεῖρας βαλεῖν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.310, 7.142; περὶ γόνυ χέρας ἱκεσίους ἔβαλον 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1414, compare “Ph.” 1622, etc.; τὰ σὰ γούναθ᾽ ἱκάνομαι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.457, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.147, etc.; κιχανόμενοι τὰ σὰ γοῦνα ἱκόμεθ᾽ 9.266; ἀντίος ἤλυθε γούνων 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.463; γόνυ σὸν ἀμπίσχειν χερί 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 165; σοῖς προστίθημι γόνασιν ὠλένας [prev. author] “Andr.” 895}}; ἐς γούνατά τινι or τινος πεσεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.86, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1607; ἀμφὶ γόνυ τινὸς πίπτειν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 787}}; γόνυ τινός or πρὸς γόνυ προσπίπτειν{{Refs|[prev. work] 339, “HF” 79; γόνασί τινος προσπίπτειν [prev. author] “Or.” 1332 (but προσπίτνω σε γόνασιν}} **on my knees**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 485)}}; πίπτειν πρὸς τὰ γ. τινος, tini, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 1.19, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.198; also γούνων λίσσεσθαι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.451; ἐλλιτανεύειν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.481; γουνάζεσθαι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.345; ἄντεσθαι πρὸς τῶν γονάτων 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 710; ἱκετεῦσαι πρὸς τ. γ. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 58.70.}}
**E.2** of a sitting posture, φημί μιν ἀσπασίως γ. κάμψειν will be glad to bend **the knee** so as to sit down and rest, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.118, compare 19.72}}; but also, bow **the knee** in submission, ἐμοὶ κάμψει (_intransitive_) πᾶν γ. {{LXX|LXX.Isa.45.23}}; γ. ὀκλάζειν τινί{{LXX|LXX.1Ki.19.18, see entry κάμπτω: ἐπὶ γούνασι}} **on** one's **knees**, ἐπὶ γούνασι πατρός {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.500; ποτὶ γ. 5.408; γούνασιν ἐφέσσεσθαι φίλον υἱόν 9.455; σ᾽ ἐπ᾽ ἐμοῖσι.. γούνεσσι καθίσσας 9.488; τόν ῥά οἱ.. ἐπὶ γούνασι θῆκεν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.401; ἐν τοῖς γόνασί τινος στρέφεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 617b}}; πέπλον.. θεῖναι Ἀθηναίης ἐπὶ γούνασιν to lay it on her **lap** (as an offering), {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.92, compare “Schwyzer” [same place]: hence _metaphorically_, θεῶν ἐν γούνασι κεῖται}} it rests **in the lap of**.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.514, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.267, etc.}}; but ἐν γούνασιν πίτνοντα Νίκας victorious, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 2.26.}}
**E.3** of the knees as the seat of strength, ἐν δὲ βίην ὤμοισι καὶ ἐν γούνεσσιν ἔθηκε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.569}}; of swiftness, λαιψηρά γ. {{Refs|22.204, etc.; γούνατά τινος λύειν disable, kill him, 5.176}}, etc.; ὑπὸ γούνατ᾽ ἔλυσεν {{Refs|11.579}}; βλάπτειν γ. τινι, κάματος δ᾽ ὑπὸ γ. ἐδάμνα, {{Refs|7.271, 21.52}}:—_passive_, αὐτοῦ λύτο γούνατα {{Refs|21.114, etc.}}
**E.4** _metaphorically_, ἐς γόνυ βάλλειν bring down upon **the knee**, i. e. humble, conquer, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.27}}; ἐς γ. ῥίπτειν, κλίνειν, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 3.20, 30; ἐς γ. ἐλθεῖν 6th c.AD: Procopius Caesariensis Historicus “Historia Arcana” 14, “Pers.” 1.17; Ἀσία δὲ χθὼν.. ἐπὶ γόνυ κέκλιται 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 931 (Lyric poetry).}}
**E.5** proverbial, ἀπωτέρω ἢ γόνυ κνάμα 'blood is thicker than water', 'charity begins at home', {{Refs 4th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 16.18; γ. κνήμης ἔγγιον 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1168b8, 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 9.383b.}}
**E.II** **joint** of grasses or plants, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.98, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.5.26, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 8.2.4, 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “de Antro Nympharum - Porphyrii Opuscula” 19}}. (Cf. Sanskrit _jā´nu_, Latin _genu_, etc.)

1120:
** γονῠ-πετέω**,
**fall on the knee**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 15.29.9, etc.}}
**2** **fall down before** one, τινά {{NT+1st c.AD+|NT.Matt.17.14, cf. NT.Mark.1.40: _absolutely_, 1st c.AD: Cornutus Philosophus “de Natura Deorum” 12.}}

1121:
** γράμμα**, ατος, τό,
_Doric dialect_ **γράθμα**, probably in {{Refs 6th c.BC+|“IG” 4.506 (Heraeum, 6th-5th c.BC),compare “Anecdota Graeca - MSS. Bibl. Oxon.” 1.102}}, but **γράσσμα**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 4.554 (Argos, 5th c.BC)}}: late _Aeolic dialect_ _plural_ **γρόππατα**, {{Refs|“epigram Gr.” 990.11 (from Balbilla)}}: (γράφω):— **that which is drawn**: _plural_, **lines of a drawing, picture**, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1146 (of tapestry), 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 15.81}}; **picture**, Ἀπέλλεω γ. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 4.73, compare “Anthologia Graeca” 6.352 (from Erinna)}}: _singular_, **drawing, picture**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 472d, “Cra.” 430e, compare 431c}}: _plural_, **figures** in a picture, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Procopius Gazaeus Rhetor “ἔκφρασις ὡρολογίου” p.157B.}}
**II** **written character, letter**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.139, 148, etc.}}: in _plural_, **letters, characters**, γραμμάτων τε συνθέσεις {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 460; πηλίκοις γ. NT.Gal.6.11}}; **the letters, the alphabet**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.58; τὰ γ. καὶ τὰς συλλαβάς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 390e; γ. Φοινίκια 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 514}}; Ἀσσύρια, Ἑλληνικά, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.87; γράμματα ἐπίστασθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 689d}}; μαθεῖν to have learnt **to read**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Prt.” 325e; γ. μὴ εἰδέναι “SIG” 2844.6}}; ἐδίδασκες γράμματα, ἐγὼ δ᾽ ἐφοίτων you **kept school**—I went there, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.265; ἤτοι τέθνηκεν ἢ διδάσκει γ. “Comica Adespota” 20; παιδεύειν γράμματα 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1337b24}}; τέχνη ἡμῶν γ. our profession is that of the **scribe,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|PTeb. 316.16 (1st c.AD).}}
**II.b** **articulate sound, letter**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 18c; τὰ γ. πάθη ἐστὶ τῆς φωνῆς 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 895a12}}; γράμματα φθέγγεσθαι{{Refs|[prev. work] 8, compare “PA” 660a5.}}
**II.c** παρὰ γράμμα λέγοντα.. σκοπεῖν **etymologically**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “MM” 1185b39}}; τὰ παρὰ γ. σκώμματα puns, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Rh.” 1412a28}}; but ἀρετὴν παρὰ γ. διώκοντες, with _reference_ to Νικαρέτη, the mistress of Stilpo, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Crates Thebanus Poeta Philosophus 1.}}
**II.d** **inscription**, τὸ Δελφικὸν γ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 229e, compare “Chrm.” 164d, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.2.24, etc., “IG” 2.2876, +others: proverbial, εἰς πέλαγος.. γράμματα γράψαι “epigram Gr.” 1038.8 (from Attalia).}}
**II.2** in _plural_, **notes** in music, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 11.78 (1st c.AD: Lucillius Epigrammaticus).}}
**II.3** mathematical **diagram**, epigram cited in {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 8.12.}}
**II.4** **letter** inscribed on the lots which the δικασταί drew, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 277, +others, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 64.4}}; practically, = **division** of dicasts, ἐν ὁποίῳ γ. δειπνεῖ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 683}}; ἁ κατὰ γράμμα φυλακά the **roster** of guards, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“SIG” 569.21 (Cos, 3rd c.BC).}}
**II.4.b** **quarters** of a town, {{Refs|“PRein.” 49.2}}; **accent,** {{Refs|EM 240.42, 12th c.AD: Zonaras Legal icographus}}
**II.5** a small weight, 1/24 **ounce, scruple**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Andromachus Poeta Medicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 13.114, “Geoponica” 7.13.2, “PLips.” 62 ii 27 (4th c.AD).}}
**III** in _plural_, **set of written characters, piece of writing**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.124}}: hence, **letter**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 5.14, “IG” 22.103.8, etc.; γραμμάτων πτυχαί 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 144, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 594, +others, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epistulae” 347c}}; **inscription, epitaph**, etc., ἐκόλαψε ἐς τὸν τάφον γράμματα λέγοντα τάδε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.187, compare 4.91, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 3.12, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 18.47, “IG” 3.751.}}
**III.2** **papers, documents**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.30, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 36.21, etc. (_sing._, 1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 65.14)}}; τούτων τὰ γ. the **documents to prove** this, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 32.14; τὰ γ. τῆς δίκης 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 772}}; τὰ δημόσια γ. the public **records**, Decrees cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.55}}; **title-deeds**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 65.14}}; **account** of loans, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 49.59; ἐπικαρπίαν ἐν γράμμασιν ἀποφέρειν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 955d}}; **contract** or **estimate**, {{Refs|“BCH” 46.323 (from Teos)}}; **catalogue**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.4.12}}: in _singular_, **bond, **{{NT|NT.Luke.16.7}}; **note of hand**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 18.6.3.}}
**III.3** a man's **writings**, i.e. **book, treatise**, τὰ τοῦ Ζήνωνος γ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Parmenides” 127c (but _sing._, [prev. work] 128a)}}: _plural_, **books**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.2.1; Πλάτωνος τὸ περὶ ψυχῆς γ. 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Epigrammata” 25, compare “Anthologia Graeca” 9.63 (3rd c.BC: Asclepiades Epigrammaticus), 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 18(2).928}}; τὰ ἱερὰ γ. the Holy {{NT+3rd c.BC+|“Scriptures”, “OGI” 56.36 (3rd c.BC), 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.574, NT.2Tim.3.15, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “contra Apionem” 1.10}}; ἱερὰ γ., = Imperial **rescripts,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|Inscriptiones Graecae 12(5).132 (Paros, 3rd c.AD)}}; = **hieroglyphics**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“OGI” 90.54 (Rosetta, 2nd c.BC)}}: in _singular_, the {{NT+2nd c.AD+|“Law” of 2nd c.AD(?): Moses Alchemista, NT.Rom.2.27, +others}}; opposed to πνεῦμα, {{Refs|[prev. work]29: _sing._}}**article** of a treaty, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.29.}}
**III.4** **laws** or **rules**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 425b, “Plt.” 292a, +others, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 1050}}; κατὰ γράμματα ἄρχειν, opposed to ἄνευ γραμμάτων, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 293a; ἡ κατὰ γ. καὶ νόμους πολιτεία 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1286a15, compare 1272a38: οἱ κατὰ γ. νόμοι, opposed to οἱ κατὰ τὰ ἔθη, [prev. work] 1287b5, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 299d; κατὰ γράμματα ἰατρεύεσθαι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1287a34; ἡ ἐκ τῶν γ. θεραπεία [prev. work] 40.}}
**IV** in _plural_, also, **letters, learning**, ἀπείρους γραμμάτων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 26d, etc.}}

1122:
** γραμμᾰτ-εύς**, έως, ὁ,
**secretary, registrar**, title of officials at Athens and elsewhere, {{NT+5th c.BC+|“IG” 12.15, etc.; ὁ γ. ὁ τῆς πόλεως 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.10; γ. τοῦ δήμου “OGI” 493.10 (Ephesus, 2nd c.AD), NT.Act.19.35; γ. ἀνδραπόδων “PHib.” 29.7; γ. τῶν μαχίμων “UPZ” 110.145 (2nd c.BC)}}; γ. τοῦ θεοῦ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 9(2).1109.21 (5th c.BC: Magnes Comicus Thess.)}}; also of subordinate officials, **clerk**, sometimes a term of contempt, ὄλεθρος γ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.127; θεοῖς ἐχθρὸς καὶ γ. [prev. author] 19.95}}; ἡ γ., in joke, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 432.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, **recorder**, of memory, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 39a.}}
**3** **scholar**, γ. σαφής {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 358 (assuming variant).}}
**4** **one who traces** or **marks out**, of Egyptian embalmers, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.91.}}

1123:
** γραπτ-ός**, ή, όν,
**painted**, ζῷα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 128.5; τύποι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 764; κύρβις 5th c.BC: Achaeus Tragicus 19; εἰκών “SIG” 1068.21 (from Patmos): γραπτά, τά}}, **paintings**, opposed to γλυπτά, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 25.}}
**2** **marked as with letters**, ἁ γραπτὰ ὑάκινθος {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 10.28; γ. λίθος “IG” 14.1089.}}
**II** **written**, νόμοι γ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Gorgias Rhetor et Sophista “Pal.” 30, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 773e, etc.; ἀσφάλεια “PAmh.” 78.17 (2nd c.AD): γραπτά, τά}}, **rescripts, proclamations**, {{LXX|LXX.2Mac.11.15}}; **legal documents, bonds**, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Manetho Astrologus 3.214.}}

1124:
** γρᾰφ-ή**, ἡ,
**representation by means of lines**: hence,
**I** **drawing, delineation**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.36}}; κατὰ γραφήν in **outline**, conjecture in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 193a}}; also of **painting**, γραφῇ κοσμέειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.24; εἰκὼν γραφῇ εἰκασμένη [prev. author] 2.182}}; **the art of drawing** or **painting**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 277c, “Ti.” 19b.}}
**I.2** **that which is drawn** or **painted, drawing, picture**, ὅσον γραφῇ only **in a picture**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.73; πρέπουσά θ᾽ ὡς ἐν γραφαῖς 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 242 (Lyric poetry)}}; σπόγγος ὤλεσεν γραφήν{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1329; μήτε ἄγαλμα μήτε γ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1336b15}}; also of embroidery, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 232; γραφαὶ ἀπὸ κερκίδος 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Philostrati majoris imagines” 2.5.}}
**I.3** γ. παρειῶν **painting, rouging** the cheeks, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ep.” 22.}}
**II** **writing** or **the art of writing**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 274b, etc.: _plural_, αἱ γ. τῶν δικῶν}} **the registration** of.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1321b36; γραφαὶ περὶ συμμαχίας, of treaties, [prev. work] 1280a40.}}
**II.2** **that which is written, writing**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 683, 5th c.BC: Agatho Tragicus 4}}: hence, of various written documents, **letter**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.129}}: also in _plural_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 735}}; ψευδεῖς γ. spurious **documents**, cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.55 (but in 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1311}} false **statements**); of published **writings**, τῶν φιλοσόφων {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” p.73 W., compare 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Oratoribus Veteribus” 4}}; ἐν τῇ πρώτῃ γ. in the first **book**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Nat.Herc.” 1431.16}}; **written law**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 934c}}; **contract,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|PAmh. 2.43.13 (2nd c.BC)}}: _plural_, **copies** of judgements delivered in court, {{Refs|“IG” 12(2).526d8 (from Eresos).}}
**II.2.b** **catalogue, list, return**, ἱερῶν {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PTeb.” 88.2 (2nd c.BC)}}; τοῦ κατ᾽ ἄνδρα {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“OGI” 179.21 (Egypt, 1st c.BC)}}; τὰς κατ᾽ ἄνδρα γραφάς {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PTeb.” 27.7 (2nd c.BC)}}, etc.; **price-list**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.91.}}
**II.2.c** **inscription**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.134, “IG” 12(5).679 (from Syros), “epigram Gr.” 347 (from Cios), 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 37.21.}}
**II.2.d** **MS. reading**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 1.2.25, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.430, 3rd c.AD: Alexander Aphrodisiensis Philosophus “in librum de sensu commentarium - pars” 9.29, 5th c.AD: Hermias Alexandrinus Philosophus “in Platonis Phaedrum scholia” p.154A., etc.}}
**II.3** **the Holy Scripture**,{{NT+1st c.AD+|Aristeas 155, NT.2Pet.1.20: _plural_, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.18, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “contra Apionem” 2.4, NT.Matt.21.42, +others}}: also in _singular_, of a particular passage, {{NT|NT.Act.8.32, +others}}
**II.4** γ. φαρμάκου medical **prescription**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 12.293, 13.638, 15.918.}}
**II.5** **record-office, archive,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 11(2).203 B 101 (Delos, 3rd c.BC).}}
**III** (γράφομαι) as law-term,
**III.1** **bill of indictment** in a public prosecution, λέγε, τὴν γ. αὐτὴν λαβών {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.53.}}
**III.2** **criminal prosecution** in the interest of the state {{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare (2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 8.41), γραφὴν ὕβρεως καὶ δίκην κακηγορίας ἰδίαν φεύξεται [prev. author] 21.32, compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 1.44, 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 11.28, etc.; γραφὴν γράφεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 929e, etc.; γρ. γ. τινά [prev. author] “Euthphr.” 2b, etc.; γ. ἀπενεγκεῖν 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.217; γραφήν τινος διώκειν τινά 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.293; πολλὰς γ. διώξας οὐδεμίαν εἷλεν 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.1.5; γραφὴν ἁλῶναι [prev. author] 2.2.9}}; γ. κατασκευάζειν κατά τινος, ἐπί τινα, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.103, 22.2}}; γ. εἰσέρχεσθαι, εἰσιέναι, appear before the court **in a public prosecution**, either as prosecutor or prosecuted, {{Refs|[prev. author] 18.105.}}
**III.3** generally, **an ordinary public action**, opposed to to special forms (such as εἰσαγγελία, εὔθυναι, etc.), γραφάς, εὐθύνας, εἰσαγγελίας, πάντα ταῦτ᾽ ἐπαγόντων μοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.249, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Atheniensium” 3.2, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 16.12.}}

1125:
** γράφω** [ᾰ],
_future_ **-ψω**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.95, etc.:}}_aorist_ ἔγραψα, _Epic dialect_ γράψα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.599}}: _perfect_ γέγραφα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 124, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.26, etc.; later γεγράφηκα “PHib.” 1.78.2 (3rd c.BC)}}:—_middle_, _future_ γράψομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 107, etc. (but in _passive_ sense, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “προτρεπτικός - Scripta Minora” 13)}}: _aorist_ ἐγραψάμην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 894, etc.}}:—_passive_, _future_ γρᾰφήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης ὀξέων” 26, 3rd c.BC: Nicomachus Comicus 1.39, (μετεγ-) 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1370; more frequently γεγράψομαι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 411, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 18.47}}, etc.: _aorist_ ἐγράφην [ᾰ], {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.91, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Parmenides” 128c, etc.; ἐγράφθην “SIG” 57.5 (Milet., 5th c.BC), 3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “περὶ τῶν ὀχουμένων” 2.4}}: _perfect_ γέγραμμαι (also in _middle_ sense, see near the end), _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ἔγραπται {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Oppianus Apamensis Epicus “Cynegetica” 3.274}}; _participle_ ἐγραμμένος or ἠγρ- {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“SIG” 9 (Elis, uncertain), “Leg.Gort.” 1.45, +others; later γεγράφημαι 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.637}}: _3rd.pers._ _plural_ γεγράφαται {{Refs|“IG” 12.57.10}}, _Doric dialect_ γεγράβανται {{Refs|“Schwyzer” 90.12 (from Argos)}}: _pluperfect_ ἐγέγραπτο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.64}}: _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἐγεγράφατο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 56.32. Used by 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} only in _aorist_ _active_:—**scratch, graze**, αἰχμὴ γράψεν οἱ ὀστέον ἄχρις {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.599}}; γράψας ἐν πίνακι πτυκτῷ θυμοφθόρα πολλά **having marked** or **drawn** signs thereon, {{Refs|6.169: hence, later}}**represent by lines, draw, paint**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.41, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 50, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 377e}}; γῆς περιόδους γ. **draw** maps, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.36; γ. Ἔρωθ᾽ ὑπόπτερον 4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 41.1; προσπεπατταλευμένον γ. τὸν Προμηθέα 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 535.2; ἀνδριάντα γ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 420c}}; ζῷα γ., ={ζωγραφεῖν} (which see), {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. author] “Grg.” 453c: _metaphorically_, ὁπόσα τοὺς λειμῶνας αἱ ὧραι γράφουσι 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Im.Praef.”}}:—_middle_, ζῷα γράφεσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.88}}:—_passive_, εἰκὼν γεγραμμένη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 537; πίνακες γεγραμμένοι τὰ Ἀλεξάνδρου ἔργα 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 2.20.}}
**2** in Mathematics texts, **describe** a figure, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Euclides Geometra “Post.” 3, +others, 3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “περὶ σφαίρας καὶ κυλίνδρου” 1.23, +others, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 1.47.}}
**2.b** of a point or line in motion, **generate** a figure, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Mechanica” 848b10, +others; τὸ σαμεῖον ἕλικα γράψει 3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “περὶ σφαίρας καὶ κυλίνδρου” 1, compare 3rd-2nd c.BC: Apollonius Pergaeus Geometra “Conica” 1.2, 2nd-1st c.BC(?): Hero Mechanicus “Automatopoetica” 8.1.}}
**3** **brand, mark**, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Oppianus Apamensis Epicus “Cynegetica” 1.326:}} —_passive_ in form γεγράφαται{{Refs|[prev. work] 322.}}
**II** **express by written characters, write**, τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.125, etc.; γ. διαθήκην 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 923c, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.5.34 (_passive_)}}; γ. τινὶ ὅτι.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.14}}; γ. τινί, with _infinitive_, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“SIG” 552.13 (Abae, 3rd c.BC)}}; γ. τι εἰς διφθέρας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.58: proverbial, ὅρκους.. γυναικὸς εἰς ὕδωρ γράφω 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 811, compare 4th c.BC: Xenarchus Comicus 6; εἰς τέφραν γ. 5th c.BC: Philonides Comicus 7}}; εἰς ὕδωρ, ἐν ὕδατι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Μονόστιχοι - Fragmenta Comicorum Graecorum” 25, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 276c; ἐν χρυσῷ πίνακι [prev. author] “Criti.” 120c; ἐν φλοιῷ 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 18.47; καθ᾽ ὕδατος 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Cat.” 21; εἰς πέλαγος γράμματα γράψαι “epigram Gr.” 1038.8 (from Attalia):}} —_passive_, πόθι φρενὸς γέγραπται in what leaf of memory **it is written**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 10(11).3.}}
**II.2** **inscribe**, γ. εἰς σκῦλα, εἰς στήλην, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 574, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 9.41:}} —_passive_, γράφεσθαί τι **to be inscribed with** a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 157; ὧδε γέγραμμαι}}**have my name inscribed,** {{Refs|Inscriptiones Graecae 12(7).3 * (uncertain)}}; ἐν τῷ προσώπῳ γραφεὶς τὴν συμφοράν **having** it **branded** on his forehead, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 854d}}; γεγραμμένα κωκύουσαν, of the hyacinth, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Euphorio Epicus 40.}}
**II.3** **write down**, γ. τινὰ αἴτιον **set** him **down** as the cause, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.214}}; γ. τι ἱερόν τινι **register** as.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 3.30}}; in magic, **invoke a curse upon,** {{Refs|Tab.Defix.Aud. 14 A 1}}; γ. τινὰ κληρονόμον, ἐπίτροπον, **institute by a written document**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 923c, 924a}}; **register, enrol**, ἐμὲ γράφε τῶν ἱππεύειν ὑπερεπιθυμούντων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.3.21}}; οὐ Κρέοντος προστάτου γεγράψομαι, as a dependent of Cr., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 411.}}
**II.4** γ. τινί **write a letter to** one, γ. σοὶ ἵνα εἰδῇς {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PGrenf.” 1.11 ii 21 (2nd c.BC)}}, etc.; εἴς τινα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Syr.D.” 23.}}
**II.5** γ. περί τίνος **write on** a subject, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 13.2, etc.; ὑπέρ τινος 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.1.4, etc.}}; εἴς τινα **against**.., {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Longinus Rhetor 4.3}}; πρός τινα **address a work** to.., {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.3}}; **describe**, οἱ ὑφ᾽ ἡμῶν γραφόμενοι καιροί {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.56.4}}; especially of Prose, opposed to ποιεῖν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 2.48: with double _accusative_, τί.. γράψειειν ἄν σε μουσοποιὸς ἐν τάφ; 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 1189.}}
**II.6** **write down** a law to be proposed: hence, **propose, move**, γνώμην, νόμον, ψήφισμα, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.7.34, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1429, etc.: _absolutely_ (i.e. νόμον), 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.179; γ. καὶ νομοθετεῖν περί τινος [prev. author] 24.48}}; γ. πόλεμον, εἰρήνην, {{Refs|[prev. author] 10.55, 19.55}}: with _infinitive_, σὺ γράφεις ταῦτ᾽ εἶναι στρατιωτικά {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.19; ἔγραψα.. ἀποπλεῖν.. τοὺς πρέσβεις [prev. author] 18.25}}; **enact**, νόμοι οὓς τὸ πλῆθος συνελθὸν ἔγραψε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.42}}:—_passive_, παρὰ τὰ γραφέντα δρᾶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 295d; τὸ γεγραμμένον ὑπὸ σοῦ ψήφισμα 4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 1.70.}}
**II.7** **prescribe, ordain**, πότμος ἔγραψε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 6.7.}}
**II.8** ὁ γράφων τὸν Ὀξυρυγχίτην (i.e. νομόν) the **secretary for** the nome of Oxyrhynchus, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“POxy.” 239.1 (1st c.AD)}}; τῷ ἰδίῳ λόγῳ γράφοντι τὸν νομόν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PFlor.” 358.5 (2nd c.AD).}}
**B** _middle_, **write for oneself** or **for one's own use, note down**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.82, “IG” 12.57.39, etc.; γ. τι ἐν φρεσίν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 450 (Lyric poetry); φρενῶν ἔσω 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1325}}; ἐγραψάμην ὑπομνήματα I **wrote me down** some memoranda, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 143a}}; **cause to be written**, συγγραφήν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 56.6, etc.}}; γ. πρόσοδον πρὸς τὴν βουλήν **petition for** a hearing before the Council, {{Refs|[prev. author] 24.48.}}
**B.2** **enrol oneself**, γράψασθαι φυλῆς καὶ δήμου καὶ φρατρίας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 12.374.16, [prev. work]2.115b21: _absolutely_, of colonists, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 850b}}; but also {{Refs 4th c.BC+|compare (4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 11.3), ἕνα τῶν μαθητῶν ἐμὲ γράφου}} **enrol** me as one of **your** disciples, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cra.” 428b.}}
**B.3** as law-term, γ. τινά **to indict** one, τινός **for** some public offence, e.g. τῆς αἰσχροκερδείας, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 754e; γ. [τινὰ] παρανόμων 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.13; in full, γραφὴν γράψασθαί τινα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1482 (but in _passive_, εἴ σοι γράφοιτο δίκη [prev. work] 758); γράψασθαι δίκας “SIG” 344.38 (from Teos)}}: with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, γ. τινὰ ἀδικεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 894, compare “Pax” 107: _absolutely_, οἱ γραψάμενοι}} **the prosecutors**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “V.” 881; ἑτέροις οὐκ ἦν γράψασθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.75}}; also γράφεσθαί τι **indict** an act, i. e. the doer of it, as criminal, ἐγράψατο τὴν Χαβρίου δωρειάν he **brought a** γραφὴ παρανόμων **against** the person who proposed the grant to Chabrias, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.146, compare 95}}; τὸ χάριν τούτων ἀποδοῦναι παρανόμων γράφει (_2nd pers._ _singular_) {{Refs|[prev. author] 18.119.}}
**B.3.b** _passive_, **to be indicted**, γραφεὶς ἀπέφυγον {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.103}}; τοῦτο τὸ ψήφισμα ἐγράφη παρανόμων **was indicted** as illegal, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.62}}; ψηφίσματα ὑπὸ τούτου οὐδὲ γραφέντα not even **indicted**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.222 (but in 18.28, εἰ μὴ τοῦτ᾽ ἐγράφη}} if this decree **had** not **been proposed**, as _passive_ of {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 11.6)}}; τὰ γεγραμμένα **the articles of the indictment**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 18.56}}; τὸ γεγραμμένον **the penalty named in the indiclment**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 24.83:}} —but γέγραμμαι usually takes the sense of the _middle_, **indict**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 18.59, 119, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 2b, “Tht.” 210d.}}

1128:
** γυμν-άζω**,
_future_ -άσω: _aorist_ ἐγύμνασα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 540}}: _perfect_ γεγύμνακα {{Refs|[prev. author] “Pr.” 586}} (Lyric poetry):—_middle_, (see. below):—_passive_, _aorist_ ἐγυμνάσθην [{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator] 61.43}}: _perfect_ γεγύμνασμαι (see. below): (γυμνός):—**train naked, train in gymnastic exercise**: generally, **train, exercise,** τὸ σῶμα, τὴν ψυχήν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 2.11; ἑαυτὸν καὶ τοὺς ἵππους 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.2.7; ἑαυτὸν πρός τι 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 2.18.27: with}}_infinitive_, γ. τοὺς παῖδάς τι ποιεῖν **train** or **accustom** them **to** do a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.32}}; γ. τινά τινι **accustom** him **to** it,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1.2.10; τινὰ περί τι 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 10.5}}; **teach rhetoric,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 2.50 S}}.:—_middle_, **exercise for oneself, practise,** γυμνάσασθαι τέχνην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 514e; τὰ περὶ τὰς διαίτας 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 14.2.19; γυμνάσιον τὸ εἰωθός 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 5.6}}; **practise gymnastic exercises,** {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 1335, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.208, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.6. etc.; δρόμῳ “IG” 4.955.8 (2nd c.AD)}}, etc.; generally, **practise,** ναῦς -ομένας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.1.16}}; of a disputer, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Topica” 108a13, etc.:}} —_passive_, ὁ γεγυμνασμένος **the trained** or **practised** orator, opposed to ὁ εὐφυής, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Rh.” 1410b8}}; γεγυμνάσθαι πρός τι, ἔντινι, **be trained** or **practised for** or **in** a thing, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 626b, 635c; περὶ τὰ ὅπλα γυμνάζεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.5.23: with _accusative_, τὰ πρὸς τὰς πολεμικὰς πράξεις γεγυμνασμένοι τὰς ἕξεις.. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1319a22; θήραν 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 3.9: with _genitive_, γεγ. θαλάττης, πολέμων, σοφίας, [prev. author] “Her.” 2.15, 3.1, 10.1; καρδία γεγ. πλεονεξίας τινί NT.2Pet.2.14; also ὕδωρ ὑπὸ συνεχῶν πληγῶν γεγ. καὶ κεκαθαρμένον 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 3.1.2.}}
**2** **prepare,** βιβλίδιον {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“PFlor.” 338.4 (3rd c.AD).}}
**II** _metaphorically_, **wear out, harass,** ἄδην με.. πλάναι γεγυμνάκασι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 586; ἔρως πατρῴας τῆσδε γῆς σ᾽ ἐγύμνασε [prev. author] “Ag.” 540}}; κρυμὸς.. πλευρὰ γυμνάζει χολῆς, of pleurisy, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 682}}:—_passive_, τοὺς ὑπερμήκεις δρόμους.. γυμνάζεται {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 592.}}
**II.2** **investigate,** {{Refs 6th c.AD+|Sammelb. 5941.12 (_passive_, 6th c.AD).}}
**III** ={γυμνόω}, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|“PSI” 1.70 (_passive_, 6th c.AD).}}

1129:
** γυμν-ᾰσία**, ἡ,
**right to use** γυμνάσιον, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1297a17 (assuming variant)}}; **exercise,** σωματικὴ γ. {{NT+2nd c.AD+|NT.1Tim.4.8: _plural_, “IG” 22.1006.65, “SIG” 1073.19 (Olympia, 2nd c.AD)}}; of **military exercises,** ἡ ἐν τοῖς ὅπλοις γ. {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.7.6}}; generally, **struggle,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 3.2.7; αἱ καθ᾽ ἡμέραν γ.}}**lessons,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 20}}: _metaphorically_ of **mental exercise,** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de communi mathematica scientia” 24}}; frequently of disputation, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 169c, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Topica” 101a27, +others}}; **training,** γ. πρὸς τὰς πολιτικὰς πράξεις {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.1.2.}}
**2** Rhet., **practice**: hence, **arrangement, disposition,** τοῦ διηγήματος Theo {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“Prog.” 4, compare 4th-5th c.AD: Aphthonius Rhetor “Προγυμνάσματα - Rhet.” 6.}}

1130:
** γυμνητ-εύω**,
**to be naked,** {{NT|NT.1Cor.4.11, Demophilus Gnomologus “Sententiae - Opuscula Graecorum Veterum Sententiosa” 8.}}
**2** **to be lightly clad,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 25.3.}}
**3** **to be light-armed,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Aemilius Paulus” 16.}}

1131:
** γυμνός**, ή, όν,
**naked, unclad,** γ. περ ἐών {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.136, etc.; τὰ γ. 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 4.4}}: _comparative_, Ἴρου γυμνότερος {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Procopius Gazaeus Rhetor “Epistulae - Epistolographi” 122}}; γυμνὸν στάδιον, opposed to ὁπλιτοδρόμος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 11.49.}}
**2** **unarmed,** οὐδ᾽ ὑπέμεινε Πάτροκλον, γυμνόν περ ἐόντ᾽ ἐν δηϊοτῆτι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.815, etc.; γυμνὰ τὰ νῶτα παρέχειν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Fabius Maximus” 11; τὰ γυμνά}}**parts not covered by armour, exposed parts,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.23, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.4.12}}; especially **right side** (the left being covered by the shields), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.10.71.}}
**3** of things **bare,** γ. τόξον an **uncovered**bow,i.e. taken out of the case, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.607; γ.ὀϊστός 21.417; γ. μάχαιραι 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 22.146; ξίφος 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 1.1254; γ. τῇ κεφαλῇ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 243b.}}
**4** c._genitive_, **stripped of** a thing, κολεοῦ γ. φάσγανον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 1.52, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 2.14; κᾶπος [δένδρων] γ. 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 3.24; γ. ὀστράκων 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 337; γ. προπομπῶν [prev. author] “Pers.” 1036 (Lyric poetry); (but also γ. τῶν ἀριστείων ἄτερ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 464): in Prose, γ. ὅπλων 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.141 (variant); ἡ ψυχὴ γ. τοῦ σώματος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 403b, compare “R.” 577b, “Grg.” 523d}}: _comparative_ ἀνδριάντων -ότερος {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 34.3.}}
**5** **lightly clad,** i.e. **in the undergarment only,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 391, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 498, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 474a, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Herm.” 23; μικροῦ γ. ἐν τῷ χιτωνίσκῳ 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.216}}; of horses, **without harness,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Cynegeticus” 24.3.}}
**6** of facts, **naked, bald,** γυμνῶν τῶν πραγμάτων θεωρουμένων {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.76; γ. τὸ ἔργον διηγήσασθαι 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tox.” 42; γυμνοτέροις χρήσασθαι τοῖς ὀνόμασιν 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.5; γ. χρῆσθαι τῇ μιμήσει 4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 112.}} _adverb_ -ῶς **baldly,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|Scholia 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 740.}}
**7** **destitute**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PSI” 6.605.4 (3rd c.BC), etc.}}
**8** **bare, mere,** κόκκος {{NT|NT.1Cor.15.37.}}
**9** **beardless,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 2.707.}}
**10** **scalped,** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 161.}}
**11** γυμνή· ἄνηβος, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}
**12** proverbial of impossibilities, γυμνῷ φυλακὴν ἐπιτάττεις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 144, 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 12}}. (Akin to Sanskrit _nagnás,_ Latin _nādus,_ etc.; compare λυγνός.)

1132:
** γυμνότης**, ητος, ἡ,
**nakedness,** {{LXX+NT+1st c.AD+|LXX.Deu.28.48, NT.Rom.8.35, 2nd c.AD: Marcus Antoninus Imperator 10.27; γ.ψυχική 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.77.}}
**2** **bare statement,** τῶν προτάσεων {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “Ars Rhetorica” 10.6.}}

1134:
** γῠναικ-εῖος**, α, ον
{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 630 (Lyric poetry), also ος, ον [prev. work] 878, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 233 (Lyric poetry)}}: _Ionic dialect_ **γῠναικ-ήιος**, η, ον: (γυνή):— **of** or **belonging to women, feminine,** γυναικεῖαι βουλαί **a woman's** designs, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.437; λουτρόν 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 753; ἔργα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.114; κόσμος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 373c}}; σκεῦος (i.e. **woman**) {{NT|NT.1Pet.3.7}}; γ.αἰδοῖον, τόποι, χῶροι, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “de Usu Partium” 15.3, 2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “περὶ αἰτιῶν καὶ σημείων ὀξέων παθῶν” 2.11, “CA” 2.10}}; **κόλπος** (={αἰδοῖον}) {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.16; ῥοῦς}}**leucorrhoea,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] 2.43; γονόρροια 2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “περὶ αἰτιῶν καὶ σημείων χρονίων παθῶν” 2.11; ἰατρός 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 2.3; γ. ἀγορά 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 2.9}}; ἡγ. θεός, = Latin _bona dea,_ {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Caesar” 9, “Cic.” 19}}; γ. πόλεμος war **with women,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|Anthologia Graeca 7.352 (1st c.BC: Meleager Epigrammaticus (?)).}}
**2** in bad sense, **womanish, effeminate,** πένθος {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 9.10; δράματα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 151; μαθήματα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Alcaeus1.127a; γ. καὶ σμικρὰ διάνοια [prev. author] “R.” 469d}}. _adverb_ -είως, πικραίνεσθαι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. author] “Lg.” 731d; ἐμπικραίνεσθαι 4th c.AD: Eusebius Myndius Philosophus 54; διακεῖσθαι 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 38.18.}}
**II** as _substantive_,
**II.1** ἡ γυναικεία, _Ionic dialect_ -ηΐη, ={γυναικών}, **part of the house reserved for the women,** {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.20, LXX.Tob.2.11.}}
**II.1.b** ἡ γ. (i.e. ἀγορά), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 22.10.}}
**II.2** τὰ γυναικεῖα **partes muliebres,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 1.26.ε᾽, 2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “περὶ αἰτιῶν καὶ σημείων ὀξέων παθῶν” 2.11.}}
**II.2.b** ={τὰ καταμήνια}, {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 5.28, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 648a31, +others, LXX.Gen.18.11.}}
**II.2.c** **lochia,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 17(2).817.}}
**II.2.d** **female disorders,** title of works by {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus and 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 4.8.6, 2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “ὀξέων νούσων θεραπευτικόν” 1.3.}}
**II.2.e** (i.e. φάρμακα) **remedies for female complaints,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “γυναικεῖα” 1.64.}}
**II.2.f** **women's garments**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PSI” 4.341.7 (3rd c.BC).}}
**II.3** γυναικεῖον, τό, ={στίβι}, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 5.84.}}

1135:
** γῠνή**,
_Doric dialect_ **γυνά**, _Boeotian dialect_ **βανά** (see entry), ἡ, _genitive_ γυναικός, _accusative_ γυναῖκα, _vocative_ γύναι (γυνή {{Refs 6th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Comicus 32): dual γυναῐκε 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 61: _plural_ γυναῖκες, γυναικῶν, etc. (as if from γύναιξ which is only found in Grammars, cf. Hdn.Gr. 2.643): _genitive_ γυναικείων 6th c.BC: Phocylides Lyricus 3 (assuming variant)}}: _Aeolic dialect_ _dative_ _plural_ γυναίκεσσι {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 7.6: Comedy texts _accusative_ γυνήν 5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 91: _plural_ _nominative_ γυναί 4th-3rd c.BC: Philippides Comicus 2, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 484, _accusative_ γυνάς “Comica Adespota” 1336, compare “Etymologicum Magnum” 243.24, “Anecdota Graeca - Bekker” 86}}:—**woman,** opposed to **man,**{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.683, etc.: with a second}} _substantive_, γ. ταμίη{{Refs 8th c.BC+|housekeeper, 6.390; δέσποινα 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.347}}; γρηΰς (which see), ἀλετρίς (which see), δμῳαὶ γυναῖκες {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.477, +others; Περσίδες γ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.3}}: _vocative_, as a term of respect or affection, **mistress, lady,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 290, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 15.12, etc.}}; φαντὶ γυναῖκες **the lasses** say, {{Refs|[prev. author] 20.30}}; πρὸς γυναικός like **a woman,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 592: proverbial, γ. μονωθεῖσ᾽ οὐδέν [prev. author] “Supp.” 749}}; ὅρκους γυναικὸς εἰς ὕδωρ γράφω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare (γράφω 11) 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 811; γυναιξὶ κόσμον ἡ σιγὴ φέρει [prev. author] “Aj.” 293.}}
**II** **wife, spouse,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.160, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.523, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.34, etc.; γ. καὶ παρθένοι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.2.25}}; opposed to ἑταίρα, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 3.13}}; γ. γνησία, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“PEleph.” 1.3 (4th c.BC)}}; also, **concubine,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.497.}}
**III** **mortal woman,** opposed to **goddess,**{{Refs 8th c.BC+|14.315, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.228, etc.}}
**IV** **female, mate** of animals, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1262a22 (uncertain meaning), 4th c.BC: Xenarchus Comicus 14, etc.}}— Not to be taken as _adjective_ in γυναῖκα θήσατο μαζόν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.58}}. (Cf. Ved. **gnā-** (frequently _disyllable_), Sanskrit _janis._)

1137:
** γωνία**, ἡ,
**corner, angle,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.51, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 84d, etc.}}; γ. ἐπίπεδος, στερεά, **plane, solid angle,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Euclides Geometra 1 “Def.” 8,11 “Def.” 11}}; αἱ πρὸς τῇ βάσει γ. **the angles** at the base, {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.5}}; ἡ ὑπὸ ΒΑΓ or ὑπὸ τῶν ΒΑΓ γ. **the angle** ΒΑΓ, {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.9, +others}}
**2** _metaphorically_, **corner, secluded spot,** ἐν γωνίᾳ ψιθυρίζειν {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 485d; ἐν γ. πεπραγμένον NT.Act.26.26.}}
**3** of the four **quarters** of the compass, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Ptolemaeus Mathematicus “Tetrabiblos” 29.}}
**4** **joint,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 690a13.}}
**II** **joiner's square,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 51c, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Marcellus” 19.}}
**III** **cutwater** of a bridge, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 2.8.}}
**IV** of persons, **leader, chief,** {{LXX|LXX.1Ki.14.38. (Akin to γόνυ.)}}

1139:
** δαιμον-ίζομαι**,
_middle_, ={δαιμονάω}, ἄλλος κατ᾽ ἄλλην δαιμονίζεται τύχην each one hath his own **fate appointed,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 191.}}
**II** as _passive_, **to be deified,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 173 (so explained by “Anecdota Graeca - Bekker” 90}}; _active_ in {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus).}}
**III** **to be possessed by a demon** or **evil spirit,** {{NT+1st c.AD+|NT.Matt.4.24, +others, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.706d.}}

1140:
** δαιμόνιον**, τό,
**divine Power, Divinity,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.87, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 894 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.13, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 382e, etc.; τὸ δαιμόνιον ἄρ᾽ ἢ θεὸς ἢ θεοῦ ἔργον 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1398a15, compare 1419a9; οἱ θεοὶ εἴσονται καὶ τὸ δ. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.239}}; φοβεῖσθαι μή τι δ. πράγματ᾽ ἐλαύνῃ some **fatality,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 9.54}}; τὰ τοῦ δ. the favours of **forlune,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epinomis” 992d.}}
**II** **inferior divine being,** μεταξὺ θεοῦ τε καὶ θνητοῦ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Smp.” 202e; καινὰ δ. εἰσφέρειν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.1.2, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 24c, cf. Vett. Val. 67.5, etc.}}; applied to the **'genius'** of Socrates, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.1.2, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 40a, “Tht.” 151a, “Euthphr.” 3b.}}
**II.2** **evil spirit,** δ. φαῦλα {{NT+3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 2.338, cf. LXX.Deu.32.17, LXX.Tob.3.8, NT.Matt.7.22, +others, “PMag.Lond.” 1.46.120 (4th c.AD).}}

1142:
** δαίμων**, ονος,
_vocative_ δαίμων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1480 (Lyric poetry), δαῖμον 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 2.11}}, ὁ, ἡ, **god, goddess,** of individual gods or goddesses, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.222, 3.420, etc.; δαίμονι ἶσος 5.438}}; ἐμίσγετο δαίμονι δαίμων, of Φιλίη and Νεῖκος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 59.1}}:—but more frequently of **the Divine power** (while θεός denotes a God in person), **the Deity,**{{Refs 8th c.BC+|compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.27}}; πρὸς δαίμονα against **the Divine power,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.98; σὺν δαίμονι by its grace, 11.792}}; κατὰ δαίμονα, almost, ={τύχῃ}, by **chance,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.111; τύχᾳ δαίμονος 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 8.67}}; ἄμαχος δ., i. e. Destiny, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 15.23: in _plural_, ὅτι δαίμονες θέλωσιν}} what **the Gods** ordain, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 16.117; ταῦτα δ᾽ ἐν τῷ δ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1443; ἡ τύχη καὶ ὁ δ. 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.63, compare 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.111; κατὰ δαίμονα καὶ συντυχίαν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 544.}}
**2** **the power controlling the destiny of individuals**: hence, one's **lot** or **forlune,** δτυγερὸς δέ οἱ ἔχραε δ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.396, compare 10.64; δαίμονος αἶσα κακή 11.61}}; δαίμονα δώσω I will deal thee **fate,** i.e. kill thee, {{Refs|I 1.8.166}}; frequently in Trag. of **good** or **ill fortune,** ὅταν ὁ δ. εὐροῇ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 601; δ. ἀσινής [prev. author] “Ag.” 1342 (Lyric poetry); κοινός [prev. author] “Th.” 812; γενναῖος πλὴν τοῦ δαίμονος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 76; δαίμονος σκληρότης 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.3.4; τὸν οἴακα στρέφει δ. ἑκάστῳ 4th c.BC: Anaxandrides Comicus 4.6}}; personified as **the good** or **evil genius** of a family or person, δ. τῷπλεισθενιδῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1569, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1194 (Lyric poetry); ὁ ἑκάστου δ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 107d, compare “PMag.Lond.” 121.505, 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de Mysteriis” 9.1; ὁ δ. ὁ τὴν ἡμετέραν μοῖραν λελογχώς 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 2.78; ἅπαντι δ. ἀνδρι συμπαρίσταται εὐθὺς γενομένῳ μυσταγωγὸς τοῦ βίου 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 16.2 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator; δ. ἀλάστορες [prev. author] 8D.; ὁ μέγας [τοῦ Καίσαρος] δ. 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Caesar” 69; ὁ σὸς δ. κακός [prev. passage]; ὁ βασιλέως δ. [prev. author] “Art.” 15; ἦθος ἀνθρώπῳ δ. 1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 119; Ξενοκράτης φησὶ τὴν ψυχὴν ἑκάστου εἶναι δ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Topica” 112a37.}}
**II** δαίμονες, οἱ, **souls of men of the golden age,** acting as tutelary deities, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 122, 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 1348, 6th c.BC: Phocylides Lyricus 15, 5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 115.5, etc.; θεῶν, δ., ἡρώων, τῶν ἐν Ἅιδου 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 392a: less frequently in _sing._, δαίμονι δ᾽ οἷος ἔησθα τὸ ἐργάζεσθαι ἄμεινον 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 314}}; τὸν τὲ δ. Δαρεῖον ἀγκαλεῖσθε, of the deified Darius, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 620}}; νῦν δ᾽ ἐστὶ μάκαιρα δ., of Alcestis, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 1003 (Lyric poetry), compare “IG” 12(5).305.5 (from Paros)}}: later, of **departed souls,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Luct.” 24}}; δαίμοσιν εὐσεβέσιν, = **Dis Manibus,** {{Refs|Inscriptiones Graecae 14.1683}}; so θεοὶ δ., {{Refs|[prev. work]938, +others: also}}**ghost,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 6.6.8.}}
**II.2** generally, **spiritual** or **semi-divine being** inferior to the Gods, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.415a, +others, 4th c.AD: Sallustius Philosophus 12, 5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 183, etc.}}; especially **evil spirit, demon,** {{NT+1st c.AD+|NT.Matt.8.31, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 8.2.5; φαῦλοι δ. 3rd c.AD: Alexander Aphrodisiensis Philosophus “Problemata - Physici et Medici Graeci Minores” 2.46}}; δαίμονος ἔσοδος εἰς τὸν ἄνθρωπον, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “περὶ αἰτιῶν καὶ σημείων χρονίων παθῶν” 1.4; πρᾶξις ἐκβάλλουσα δαίμονας “PMag.Par.” 1227.}}
**II.3** ἀγαθὸς δ. **the Good Genius** to whom a toast was drunk after dinner, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 525, 4th c.BC: Nicostratus Comicus 20, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 4.3, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.655e, 5th c.BC: Philonides Comicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 15.675b, 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 9.39.5, “IG” 12(3).436 (from Thera)}}, etc.; of Nero, ἀ. δ. τῆς οἰκουμένης {{Refs|“OGI” 666.3}}; of the Nile, ἀ. δ. ποταμός{{Refs 1st c.AD+|[prev. work]672.7 (1st c.AD)}}; of the tutelary genius of individuals (above 1), ἀ. δ. Ποσειδωνίου {{Refs|“SIG” 1044.9 (Halic.): _plural_, δαίμονες ἀ.}} = Latin _Di Manes_, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“SIG” 1246 (from Mylasa): Astrology texts, ἀγαθός, κακός δ., names of celestial κλῆροι, 4th c.AD: Paulus Alexandrinus Astrologus “N.” 4, “O.” 1, etc. (Less correctly written Ἀγαθοδαίμων, which see).}}
**B** ={δαήμων}, **knowing,** δ. μάχης **skilled in** fight, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 3.4. (5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 398b}}, suggests this as the originally sense; while others would write δαήμονες in {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus}}, and get rid of this sense altogether; compare however αἵμων. More probably the Root of δαίμων (**deity**) is δαίω **to distribute destinies;**;{{Refs 7th c.BC+|compare 7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 48.)}}

1143:
** δάκν-ω**,
{{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Hipponax Iambographus 49.6 (conjecture), 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 399, etc.}}: _future_ δήξομαι [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ γυναικείης φύσιος” 16, “Mul.” 1.18 (variant{δάξεται})}}]: _perfect_ δέδηχα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Babrius Fabularum Scriptor 77:}}_aorist 1_ ἔδηξα late, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Asin.” 9}}: _aorist 2_ (the only tense in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus) ἔδᾰκον Batrachomyomachia 181, 7th c.BC: Tyrtaeus Elegiacus 10.32, etc.}}, _Epic dialect_ δάκε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.493, reduplicate δέδακε “Anthologia Graeca” 12.15 (2nd c.AD: Strato Epigrammaticus)}}: _Epic dialect_ _infinitive_ δακέειν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.572}}: —_passive_, δάκνομαι {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 910}}: _future_ δηχθήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 1100}}: _aorist_ ἐδήχθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 254, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 18, etc.; later ἐδάκην 2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “περὶ αἰτιῶν καὶ σημείων χρονίων παθῶν” 2.2}}: _perfect_ δέδηγμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 1, etc.}}; _Doric dialect_ δεδαγμένος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 8.87, 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Epigrammata” 50}} codices:—**bite,** of dogs, δακέειν μὲν ἀπετρωπῶντο λεόντων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.585}}; of a gnat, ἰσχανάᾳ δακέειν {{Refs|17.572}}; στόμιον δ. **champ** the bit, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1009}}; χεῖλος ὀδοῦσι δακών, as a mark of stern determination, Tyrt.[same place]: _absolutely_, δακὼν ἀνάσχου {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Σαμία” 141}}; δ. στόμα **bite** one's tongue, so as to refrain from speaking, πρὸ τῶν τοιούτων χρὴ λόγων δ. στόμα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 397, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 976}}; δ. ἑαυτόν **to bite** one's lips for fear of laughing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 43}}; so (by a joke παρὰ προσδοκίαν) δ. θυμόν {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. author] “Nu.” 1369; δ. χόλον 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 3.1170.}}
**II** _metaphorically_ of pungent smoke and dust, **sting,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 18, “Lys.” 298, “Pl.” 822}}; δ. ὄμματα, of dry winds, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 3.17.}}
**III** of the mind, **bite, sting,** δάκε δὲ φρένας Ἕκτορι μῦθος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.493, compare 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 567; ἔδακε λύπη 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.16.a'; συμφορὰ δ. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 846}}; λόφοι δὲ κώδωντ᾽ οὐ δάκνουσ᾽ ἄνευ δορός **have** no **sting,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Th.” 399; σαίνουσα δάκνεις 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 885; τὸ δάκνον τῆς συμβουλῆς 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 7.207d}}; of love, πάντες οἱ ἐν ὥρᾳ τὸν φιλόπαιδα δάκνουσι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 474d}}:—frequently in _passive_, δηχθεῖσα κέντροις.. ἠράσθη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1303; ἔρωτι δεδαγμένος Callias Epigrammaticus}} [prev. cited]; of vexation, δάκνομαι ψυχήν {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 910}}; συμφορᾷ δεδαγμένοι Pi.[same place]; δέδηγμαι καρδίαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 1; ὑπὸ τῆς δαπάνης [prev. author] “Nu.” 12}}; πρός τι, ἐπί τινι, at a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 378, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.3.3; ὑπὸ τῶν ἐν φιλοσοφίᾳ λόγων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 218a}}: c._participle_, ἐδήχθη ἀκούσας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.13}}. (Cf.Sanskrit_dáśati_ 'bite', Gothic _tahjan_ 'tear'.)

1144:
** δάκρυ**, τό,
used by Poets for the sake of meter for δάκρυον in _singular_ δάκρυ and _dative_ _plural_ δάκρυσι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.570, etc.: _dative_ _plural_ sometimes in Prose, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.75, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 30.32, “Eu/.Luc.” 7.38: _plural_, δάκρη “Anecdota Graeca - MSS. Bibl. Oxon.” 1.121 (conjecture Bgk. in 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 122.3, compare δάκρυον 2)}}:—**tear,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.266, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.114, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 638, etc.; τοῦ ὅ γε δ. χέων 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.24.}}
**II** generally, **drop,** λιβάνου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus [same place]; δ. πεύκινον 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1200}}. (Cf.Latin _lacruma,_ Gothic _tagr,_ O{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus}} **tear.**)

1145:
** δακρύω**,
first in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 81}}: _future_ **-ύσω**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 658}}, later **-ύσομαι** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd-4th c.AD: Tryphiodorus Epicus 404}}: _aorist_ ἐδάκρῡσα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.112, etc.}}, _Epic dialect_ δάκρῡσα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.55}}: _perfect_ δεδάκρῡκα {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 2.3.14}}:—_middle_, δακρύεσθαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 815}}: _aorist_ δακρύσατο {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd-4th c.AD: Tryphiodorus Epicus 431}}:—_passive_, _present_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1226}}: _perfect_ δεδάκρῡμαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.7, etc.}}: [υ long in all tenses, except in late Poets, as [{{Refs|“Anthologia Graeca” 9.148]: (for the Root, see at {δάκρυ}).}}]
**I** _intransitive_, **weep, shed tears,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” [same place], etc.: with _accusative_ _cognate_, δ. γόους}} to lament **with tears,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 580: with _genitive_ causae, δ. συμφορᾶς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 528 (uncertain reading)}}; δ. βλέφαρα **to flood** them **with tears,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Hel.” 948; δ. χαρᾷ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.2.9; ἐπὶ ταῖς συμφοραῖς 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.168:}} — _passive_, _perfect_ δεδάκρυμαι **to be in tears,** τίπτε δεδάκρυσαι, Πατρόκλεε; {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.7; δεδάκρυνται δὲ παρειαί 22.491; δεδακρυμένος}}**in tears,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Axiochus” 364b, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Publicola” 6, etc.}}
**I.2** of the eyes, **run,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 620a5.}}
**I.3** of trees, **exude gum,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Fragmenta” 121; ἤλεκτρον δακρύειν 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Salt.” 55.}}
**II** with _accusative_, **weep for,** τινά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1490 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1486, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 1027, etc.}}:—_passive_, **to be wept for,** συμφορὰ δακρύεται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1226}} (probably _middle_ in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 815).}}

1146:
** δακτύλιος** [ῠ],
_Boeotian dialect_ **δακκύλιος** (see. δάκτυλος), ὁ, **ring, signet,** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 35, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.38, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 884, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 359e}}; ὁ ἐπὶ τοῦ δ. keeper of the **signet,** {{LXX+3rd c.BC+|LXX.Tob.1.22; ὁ τῆς πόλεως δ. “OGI” 229.88 (Smyrna, 3rd c.BC)}}; δ. φαρμακίτης {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 87, compare 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 177.}}
**II** **anything ringshaped,** as,
**II.1** **felloe** of a wheel, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 1.145.}}
**II.2** **anus,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.70, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Demon.” 17, “PRyl.” 28.68 (4th c.AD).}}
**II.3** _plural_, **movable rings** on a bit, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 10.9.}}
**II.4** **stone** to which mooring-cables were attached, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}
**II.5** **door-handle,** {{Refs|[prev. author]}}
**II.6** **end of the steering-paddle,** {{Refs|[prev. author]}}

1147:
** δάκτῠλος**, ὁ,
_poetry_ _plural_ δάκτυλα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 19.3, “Anthologia Graeca” 9.365 (4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator Imp.), also 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physiognomonica” 810a22}}: **-finger,** ἐπὶ δακτύλων συμβάλλεσθαι τοὺς μῆνας to reckon on **the fingers,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.63; ὁ μέγας δ.}}**the thumb,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 3.8, 5th c.BC: Diogenes Apolloniates Philosophus 6; ὁ μέσος 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 687b18; οἱλιχανοί 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 37; ὁ ἔσχατος [prev. author] “PA” 687b17: proverbial, ἄκρῳ δ. γεύεσθαι 5th-6th c.AD: Procopius Gazaeus Rhetor “Epistulae - Epistolographi” 31; οὐκ ἄξια ψόφου δακτύλων 4th-3rd c.BC: Clearchus Historicus 5.}}
**2** οἱ δ. τῶν ποδῶν **the toes,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.5.12}}; and, without ποδός, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Batrachomyomachia 45, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 874, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 494a12; τὸ τῶν δ. μέγεθος ἐναντίως ἔχει ἐπί τε τῶν ποδῶν καὶ τῶν χειρῶν [prev. author] “PA” 690a30}}; ὁ μέσος δ. of a monkey, {{Refs|[prev. author] “HA” 502b3}}; ὁ μείζων δ. the great **toe,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pyrrhus” 3}}. b. of **the toes** of beasts, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 498a34}}; of birds, {{Refs|[prev. author] “PA” 695a22.}}
**II** a measure of length, **finger's breadth,** = about 7/10 of an inch, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.60, +others; πώνωμεν, δάκτυλος ἀμέρα 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 41; δάκτυλος ἀώς “Anthologia Graeca” 12.50 (3rd c.BC: Asclepiades Epigrammaticus)}}: Astron., **digit,** i.e. twelfth part of the sun's or moon's apparent diameter, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Cleomedes Astronomus 2.3.}}
**III** metrical foot, **dactyl,** -?~X ?~X, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 400b; ῥυθμὸς κατὰ δάκτυλον 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 651}}; δ. κατ᾽ ἵαμβον, **diiambus,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor 1st c.AD: Quintilianus Rhetor Latinus 1.17.}}
**III.2** δάκτυλοι, οἱ, a **dance,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 14.629d.}}
**IV** **date,** fruit of the φοῖνιξ, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 342a10, 2nd c.AD: Artemidorus Daldianus Onirocriticus 5.89.}}
**IV.2** kind of **grape,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Plinius Rerum Naturalium Scriptor “Historia Naturalis” 14.15, Colum. 3.2.1.}}
**IV.3** ={ἄγρωστις}, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Plinius Rerum Naturalium Scriptor “Historia Naturalis” 24.182.}}
**V** Δάκτυλοι Ἰδαῖοι mythical **wizards** and **craftsmen** in Crete (or Phrygia, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 17.7)}}, attached to the cult of Rhea Cybele, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Fragmenta” 176, 6th c.BC: Pherecydes Lerius Historicus 47 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 364, 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 8.3.30, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 5.64, “IG” 12(9).259.22 (from Eretria).}}
**V.2** δ. Ἰδαῖοι, ={γλυκυσίδη}, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 3.140.}}
**V.2.b** fossil found in Crete, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Plinius Rerum Naturalium Scriptor “Historia Naturalis” 37.170.}}
**VI** δ. θεοῦ the **hand** of God, {{LXX+NT|LXX.Exo.8.19, cf. NT.Luke.11.20. (Orig. Δάτκυλος}} compare _Boeotian dialect_ δακκύλιος {{Refs|“Schwyzer” 462 B 51}}; δατ- = **d[ngnull]t,** cf. Sanskrit _a-datkas_ 'toothless'.)

1150:
** δαμάζω**
{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 324 (Lyric poetry)}}, etc.: _future_ δαμάσω {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 6.329 (1st c.AD: Leonidas Alexandrinus Epigrammaticus)}}; _Epic dialect_ δαμάσσω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.176, also δαμᾷ, δαμάᾳ, 1.61, 22.271}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ δαμόωσι {{Refs|6.368 (see. δαμάω)}}: _aorist 1_ ἐδάμᾰσα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 7.90}} (_participle_ δαμάσσαις {{Refs 8th c.BC+|“O.” 9.92), _Epic dialect_ ἐδάμασσα, δάμασσα, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.191, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.367}}: _perfect_ δεδάμακα {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes “Flor.Monac.” 82}}:—_middle_, _future_ _Epic dialect_ δαμάσσομαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.226:}}_aorist_ ἐδαμάσσατο, δαμάσαντο, δαμασσάμενος, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.516, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.210, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.454}}; _aorist 1_ _optative_ δαμάσαιτο {{Refs|“Leg.Gort.” 2.11}}: _aorist 2_ _optative_ δάμοιτο {{Refs|“CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 4000.18 (from Iconium)}}:—_passive_, _future_ 3 δεδμήσομαι {{Refs|“hymnus ad Apollinem” 543; irregular δαμοῦμαι “PMag.Par.” 1.2906}}: _aorist_ ἐδαμάσθην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.231, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 2.20, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 279 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 563}}; _Epic dialect_ δαμάσθην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.9, compare 16.816}}; ἐδμήθην, _imperative_ δμηθήτω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|9.158, δμηθείς 4.99, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 1000}}, _Doric dialect_ δμᾱθείς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 907 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus (Lyric poetry, see below), 3rd c.BC: Cercidas Iambographus 7.1: ἐδάμην [ᾰ] 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.812, 4th-5th c.BC: Parmenides Poeta Philosophus 7.1, etc.}}; _Epic dialect_ δάμην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.90}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ δάμεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.344}}; _Epic dialect_ _subjunctive_ δαμείω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.54, 2}} and _3rd.pers._ _singular_ δαμήῃς -ήῃ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.436, 22.246}}, _2nd pers._ _plural_ δαμήετε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|7.72; _optative_ δαμείην 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.301, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 648}}; _infinitive_ δαμῆναι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.522, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 368 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 200}}, _Epic dialect_ _infinitive_ δᾰμήμεναι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.312}}; _participle_ δαμείς {{Refs 7th c.BC+|22.40, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 90, etc.}} (only form of _aorist_ used by {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus, and preferred by 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus and 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus)}}: _perfect_ δέδμημαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.878}}, etc., **-ημένος** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|14.482, etc.; later δεδαμασμένος 2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Alexipharmaca” 29, “epigram Gr.” 550.9}}: _pluperfect_ δέδμητο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.305}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ **-ήατο**{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.183.—poetical Verb, used by 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus}} in _present_ _participle_ δαμάζων {{Refs|“Mem.” 4.3.10:}}_aorist_ _passive_ δαμασθεῖεν{{Refs|[prev. work] 4.1.3}}; also _infinitive_ δαμασθῆναι is _falsa lectio_ in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 7.4}}:—**overpower**:
**I** of animals, **tame, break in,** twice in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, in _middle_, ἡμίονον.. ἥτ᾽ ἀλγίστη δαμάσασθαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.655; τῶν κέν τιν᾽.. δαμασαίμην 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.637}}:—later in _active_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.3.10:}} —_passive_ {{Refs|[prev. work] 4.1.3.}}
**I.2** of metals, **work,** σίδαρον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 980 (Lyric poetry)}}; of land, **clear**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PSI” 4.316 (iv(?) 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus).}}
**II** of maidens, **make subject** to a husband, ἀνδρὶ δάμασσεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.432}}:—_middle_, **force, seduce,** {{Refs|Leges Gortynensium}} [same place]:—_passive_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.301, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.269.}}
**III** **subdue, conquer,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.59, +others; βίῃ καὶ χερσὶ δ. 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 490}}:—_passive_, **to be subject to** another, σοί τ᾽ ἐπιπείθονται καὶ δεδμήμεσθα ἕκαστος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.878; δέδμητο δὲ λαὸς ὑπ᾽ αὐτῷ 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.305.}}
**III.b** of the gods, **bring low,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.118, 16.845, +others}}
**III.c** **subdue, gain the mastery over,** ἐπιθυμίαν Stob. [prev. cited]
**III.2** **lay low, kill,** especially in fight, εἴ χ᾽ ὑπ᾽ ἐμοί γε θεὸς δαμάσῃ μνηστῆρας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.213}}:—_passive_, ὑπ᾽ ἐμοὶ δμηθέντα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.646; ὑπὸ δουρὶ δαμέντα [prev. work] 653.}}
**III.3** of the powers of nature, etc., **overcome, overpower,** ἔρος.. θυμὸν ἐνὶ στήθεσσιν... ἐδάμασσεν {{Refs|14.316}}:—_middle_, δαμασσάμενος φρένας οἴνῳ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.454, compare 516}}:—_passive_, **to be overcome,** αἴθρῳ καὶ καμάτῳ δεδμημένον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|14.318; μαλακῷ δεδμημένοι ὕπνῳ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.2; ὕπνῳ καὶ φιλότητι δαμείς 14.353; ἁλὶ δέδμητο φίλον κῆρ 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.454, compare 8.231; δμαθέντες}}**dead,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 127 (Lyric poetry).}}
**IV** ἀγῶνα δαμάσσαι ἔργῳ **win** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 8.80.}}
**V** οὐ μήποτε τοῦτο δαμῇ, εἶναι μὴ ἐόντα it shall never be **proved** that.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Parmenides Poeta Philosophus 7.1.}} (δᾰμᾰ-: δμη- underlies δάμνημι, ἐδάμα(σ)σα, δέδμημα; δαμάζω is a post-Homeric form of _present_; cf. Sanskrit _dā´myati_ 'to be tamed', **damitar-** 'tamer', etc.)

1151:
** δᾰμᾰλ-ις**, εως, ἡ
(ὁ {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 1.39)}}, (δαμάζω) **young cow, heifer,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 351 (Lyric poetry), 4th c.BC: Aeneas Tacticus 27.1, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 1.35}}; of Io, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 18.24, 2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Alexipharmaca” 344; also _masculine_, 5th c.BC: Hellanicus Historicus 111J.}}
**2** δ. σῦς {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“IG” 5 (1).1390.34,69 (Andania, 1st c.BC).}}
**II** **girl,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Epicrates Comicus 9, “Anthologia Graeca” 5.291 (6th c.AD: Agathias Historicus et Epigrammaticus).}}

1155:
** δᾰν-είζω**,
_future_ **-είσω**{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 35.52}}: _aorist_ ἐδάνεισα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.1.34, etc.}}: _perfect_ δεδάνεικα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 35.52:}} —_middle_ {{Refs|[prev. passage]}}: _future_ δανείσομαι {{Refs|[prev. author] 32.15}}: _aorist_ ἐδανεισάμην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.59, etc.:}}_perfect_ δεδάνεισμαι in _middle_ sense, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.5.19, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 37.53}}:—_passive_, _aorist_ ἐδανείσθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.4.28, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 33.12}}: _perfect_ δεδάνεισμαι {{Refs|[prev. author] 36.5, 49.53}}: (δάνος):—**put out money at usury, lend,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 12.302.56, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 842, +others; more fully, δ. ἐπὶ τόκῳ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 742c; ἐπὶ ὀκτὼ ὀβολοῖς τὴν μνᾶν δ. τοῦ μηνὸς ἑκάστου 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 53.13, compare 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.107}}; δ. ἐπὶ τούτοις τοῖς ἀνδραπόδοις on the security of.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.27; ἐπὶ τοῖς σώμασι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 9.1; εἰς τὰ ἡμέτερα 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.28; δανεῖσαι χρήματα εἰς τὸν Πόντον καὶ πάλιν Ἀθήναζε [prev. author] 35.3.}}
**2** _middle_, **have lent to one, borrow,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1306, etc.; ἀπό τινος 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 17.2}}; ἐπὶ τοῖς μεγάλοις [τόκοις] {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.15; δ. ἐγγείων τόκων 34.23:}} —_active_ and _middle_ opposed, ἀποδώσουσι οἱ δανεισάμενοι τοῖς δανείσασι τὸ γιγνόμενον ἀργύριον cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 35.11}}:—_passive_, of the money, **to be lent out,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 756, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.4.28, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 33.12.}}
**3** _metaphorically_ in _middle_, μόρια ἀπὸ τοῦ κόσμου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 42e; ἀποδώσετέ μοι ἃ ἐδανείσασθε ἐν τῷ λόγῳ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 612c.}}

1157:
** δαν-ειστής**, οῦ, ὁ,
**money-lender** or **creditor**, {{LXX+NT+4th c.BC+|“IPE” 12.32 B 84 (from Olbia), LXX.4Ki.4.1, NT.Luke.7.41, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.284, +others, Hierocl. p.57 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus, “POxy.” 68.25 (2nd c.AD).}}
**II** **borrower,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|Inscriptiones Graecae 12(7).67.41,68.4 (Amorg.), 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Solon” 13.}}

1159:
** δᾰπᾰν-άω**:—
_passive_, _present_ _participle_ δαπανούμενα (as if from δαπανέω) {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“IG” 5(1).1390.55 (Andania, 1st c.BC)}}: _future_ δαπανηθήσομαι {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC(?): Satyrus Epigrammaticus 20, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.218d}}: _aorist_ ἐδαπανήθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.4.11}}: _perfect_ δεδαπάνημαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.125, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 21.5, etc.:}}_pluperfect_ δεδαπάνητο (κατα-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.34}}:—_passive_, also _middle_ δαπανάομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 2.37, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 588, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus, etc.}}; _imperfect_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 569.10, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 25.13}}: _aorist_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 15.225, 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 5.43 (but -ήσας [prev. work] 45)}} (_aorist_ _middle_ ἐδαπανησάμην {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.AD: Eunapius Historicus “Fragmenta Historica - HGM” p.271 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator)}}: _perfect_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 18.63}}:— **spend,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.29, etc.; ὑπὲρ τὴν οὐσίαν δ. 4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 32.7; δ. τὰ προσταττόμενα 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.42}}; δ. εἴς τι **to spend** upon a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.45, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.3.11, probably in 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1307b34; δ. ἐκ τῶν αὑτοῦ 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 7.38}}; τἀναλώματα πάντα ἐκ τῶν ἰδίων ἐδαπανῶμεν **defrayed** all expenses, interpolated in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.154}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.125; τὰ λαμβανόμενα καὶ δαπανώμενα 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1314b5: also}}_middle_ (see. above), **spend,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.37; δ. μεγάλα 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.32, compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 33.5: with _accusative_ _cognate_, τοσαύτας δαπάνας δαπανώμενος [prev. author] 21.3; ὅσα δεδαπάνησθε εἰς τὸν πόλεμον 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.27, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 18.63}}; δαπανηθεὶς οὐδέν uncertain reading in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 5.43.}}
**2** **consume, use up,** ἡ φύσις δ. τὸν θορόν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 757a25; χρόνον εἴς τι 1st c.AD: Onosander (Onasander) Tacticus 1.5}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 745a13}}; of time, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Αιβυκή” 130}}; **destroy, consume,** φλὸξ δ. πάντα {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.208; ἄνεμος [πόλιν] ἐδαπάνησεν 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 1.94}}; of persons, **to be destroyed,** ὑπὸ θηρίων {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.43}}; καθάπερ ὑπὸ πυρός{{Refs 1st c.BC+|[prev. work] 433; πρὸς θηρίων 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 5.79; ἐν ταρτάροις καὶ βαράθροις δαπανωμένους 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 4.81}}; ὑπὸ νόσου or νόσῳ δαπανᾶσθαι {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Galba” 17, 4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Orationes” 55.27:}} —_middle_, πυρὶ καὶ φόνῳ καὶ σιδήρῳ πάντα δαπανήσασθαι Eun.[prev. cited]
**II** causal, τὴν πόλιν δαπανᾶν **to put** it **to expense, exhaust** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.3, compare 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.642.}}

1160:
** δᾰπᾰν-η**, ἡ,
**cost, expenditure,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 723, +others}}; δ. χρυσοῦ καὶ ἀργύρου, χρημάτων, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.129, 3.13}}; δ. κούφα **the cost** is little, with _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 893 (Lyric poetry); εἰς κενὸν ἡ δ. “IG” 14.1746.10}}: also in _plural_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.15; δαπάναι ἐλπίδων 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I..” 4 (5).57.}}
**II** **money spent,** ἵππων **on** horses, {{Refs|[prev. work]3(4)}}.47; δαπάνην παρέχειν **money for spending,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.41; ξυμφέρειν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.99; ὅπως μὴ ἡ εἰς τὸν ἐνιαυτὸν κειμένη δ. εἰς τὸν μῆνα δαπανᾶται 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 7.36.}}
**III** **extravagance,** ἡ ἐν τῇ φύσει δ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.218.}}

1161:
** δέ**,
**but**: adversative and copulative Particle,
**I** answering to μέν (which see), τὴν νῦν μὲν Βοιωτίαν, πρότερον δὲ Καδμηίδα γῆν καλουμένην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.12, etc.}}
**II** without preceding μέν,
**II.1** adversative, expressing distinct opposition, αἰεί τοι τὰ κάκ᾽ ἐστὶ φίλα.. μαντεύεσθαι, ἐσθλὸν δ᾽ οὔτε τί πω εἶπας{{Refs 5th c.BC+|lines 1.108; ὀρθῶς ἔλεξας, οὐ φίλως δέ μοι λέγεις 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 100, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 85, etc.; τέθνηκεν ἀνδρὸς οὐδενός, θεοῦ δ᾽ ὕπο [prev. author] “Ph.” 334}}; so in Prose, οὐκ ἐπὶ κακῷ, ἐλευθερώσει δέ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.86; οἱ αἰχμάλωτοι.. ᾤχοντο εἰς Δεκέλειαν, οἱ δ᾽ εἰς Μέγαρα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.2.14, compare “Cyr.” 4.5.46; ἡ δ᾽ ἑτέρα “IG” 2.652 A 45.}}
**II.2** copulative,
**II.2.a** in explanatory clauses, ξυνέβησαν.. τὰ μακρὰ τείχη ἑλεῖν (ἦν δὲ σταδίων μάλιστα ὀκτώ) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.66, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.48}}: when a _substantive_ is followed by words in apposition, Ἀρισταγόρῃ τῷ Μιλησίῳ, δούλῳ δὲ ἡμετέρῳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.8. β; μήτηρ βασιλέως βασίλεια δ᾽ ἐμή 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 152}}; so in answers, διπλᾶ λέγειν. —Answ. διπλᾶδ᾽ ὁρᾶν {{Refs|[prev. author] “Th.” 974.}}
**II.2.b** in enumerations or transitions, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.43 - 49, 345 - 351, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.2.1, etc.}}; with repetition of a word in different relations, ὣς Ἀχιλεὺς θάμβησεν.., θάμβησαν δὲ καὶ ἄλλοι [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.483; σάκος εἷλε.., εἵλετο δ᾽ ἔγχος 14.9 {δε}; Ζεύς ἐστιν αἰθήρ, Ζεὺς δὲ γῆ, Ζεὺς δ᾽ οὐρανός 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 70; κινεῖκραδίαν, κινεῖδὲ χόλον 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 99; ὄζει ἴων, ὄζει δὲ ῥόδων, ὄζει δ᾽ ὑακίνθου 5th c.BC: Hermippus Comicus 82.8}}]; in rhetorical outbursts, οὐκ ἂν εὐθέως εἴποιεν· τὸν δὲ βάσκανον, τὸν δὲ ὄλεθρον, τοῦτον δὲ ὑβρίζειν,—ἀναπνεῖν δέ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.209}}; in a climax, πᾶν γύναιον καὶ παιδίον καὶ θηρίον δέ **nay** even beast, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 171e, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.2.37}}; in the combination καὶ δέ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.80, +others, καὶ.. δέ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 973, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 1117, etc., each particle retains its force.}}
**II.2.c** answering to τε (which see), ἃ τῶν τε ἀποβαινόντων ἕνεκα ἄξια κεκτῆσθαι, πολὺ δὲ μᾶλλον αὐτὰ αὑτῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 367c.}}
**II.3** implying causal connexion, less direct than γάρ, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.160, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.433.}}
**II.4** in questions, with implied opposition, ἑόρακας δ᾽, ἔφη, τὴν γυναῖκ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.1.4; καὶ ὁ Σωκράτης, εἰπέ μοι, ἔφη, κύνας δὲ τρέφει; [prev. author] “Mem.” 2.9.2, compare 2.1.26, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1172: in Trag. (not in Comedy texts or Oratt.), when the speaker turns from one person to another, the _vocative_ stands first, then the _person_ _pronoun_ followed by δέ, as Μενέλαε, σοὶ δὲ τάδε λέγω.. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 622, etc.; also in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, ὦ δέσποτα, ἐγὼ δὲ ταῦτα ἐποίησα 1.115.}}
**II.4.b** τί δ; what **then**? to mark a transition in dialogue; see at {τίς}.
**II** in apodosi:
**II.1** after hypothetical clauses, εἰ δέ κε μὴ δώωσιν, ἐγὼ δέ κεν αὐτὸς ἕλωμαι if they will not give it, **then** I.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.137, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.54; εἰ μηδὲ τοῦτο βούλει ἀποκρίνασθαι, σὺ δὲ τοὐντεῦθεν λέγε 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.5.21, compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 3.43 (variant), 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1060, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.1, etc.}}
**II.1.b** after temporal or relative clauses, with ἐπεί, ἕως, etc., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.255, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.70, etc.; μέχρι.. εἶχον τὰ βέλη, οἱ δὲ ἀντεῖχον 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.98}}; with _demonstrative_ Pronouns or _adverbs_ answering to a preceding relative, οἵηπερ φύλλων γενεή, τοίη δὲ καὶ ἀνδρῶν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.146, etc.; ἆθλα οἷς κεῖται ἀρετῆς μέγιστα, τοῖς δὲ καὶ ἄνδρες ἄριστοι πολιτεύουσιν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.46; τοὺς δ᾽ ἔλαβε τυράννους.. τούτους δὲ.. ἐξεδίδου 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.37, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.98, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 194d; οἷα μὲν ἕκαστα ἐμοὶ φαίνεται τοιαῦτα μὲν ἔστιν ἐμοί, οἷα δὲ σοί, τοιαῦτα δὲ αὖ σοί [prev. author] “Tht.” 152a}}: sometimes after a participle, οἰόμενοι.. τιμῆς τεύξεσθαι, ἀντὶ δὲ τούτων οὐδ᾽ ὅμοιοι.. ἐσόμεθα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.6.16, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 15.71 (variant).}}
**II.2** to resume after an interruption or parenthesis, χρόνου δὲ ἐπιγινομένου καὶ κατεστραμμένων σχεδὸν πάντων..,—κατεστραμμένων δὲ τούτων.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.28, 29; νῦν δ᾽ αὖ πάλιν ὑπό τε πλούτου διαθρυπτόμενος.. καὶ ὑπ᾽ ἀνθρώπων.. ὑπὸ τοιούτων δέ.. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.2.23}}; with an anacoluthon, ἡ δὲ ψυχὴ ἄρα,—οἷ ἂν θεὸς θέλῃ.. τῇ ἐμῇ ψυχῇ ἰτέον, αὕτη δὲ δή.. [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 80d: for δ᾽ οὖν, see at {οὖν}.}}]
**II.3** to begin a story, ἦμος δ᾽ ἠέλιος.. **well,** when the sun.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.400.}}
**II.4** to introduce a proof, τεκμήριον δέ, σημεῖον δέ, (see entry).
**B** POSITION of δέ. It usually stands second: hence frequently between _Article_ and _substantive_ or _preposition_ and case; but also after _substantive_, or words forming a connected notion, hence it may stand third, γυναῖκα πιστὴν δ᾽ ἐν δόμοις εὕροι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 606, compare “Th.” 411, “Eu.” 531, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 959, etc.; fourth, [prev. author] “OT” 485, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 688, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 323, 383, etc.; fifth, [prev. work] 401 codices; even sixth, Epigen. 7 (codices 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus)}}; so in Prose after a _negative_, οὐχ ὑπ᾽ ἐραστοῦ δέ, to avoid confusion between οὐ δέ and οὐδέ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 227c.}}

1162:
** δέ-ησις**, εως, ἡ,
**entreaty,** {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 2.15 (_plural_), 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 8.138 (_plural_), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epistulae” 329d (_plural_), etc.; δέομαι δ᾽ ὑμῶν.. δικαίαν δέησιν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 29.4; δεήσεις ποιεῖσθαι NT.Luke.5.33, cf. Wilcken “Chr.” 41 ii 12 (3rd c.AD).}}
**2** written **petition,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|CPHerm. 6.10, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 7.5.2, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.586, “PGen.” 16.10 (3rd c.AD).}}
**II** **want, need,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Sophista 11; ἐν ἐπιθυμίαις τε καὶ δεήσεσιν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Eryxias” 405e}}; κατὰ τὰς δεήσεις according to their **needs,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1257a23; δεήσεις εἰσὶν αἱ ὀρέξεις [prev. author] “Rh.” 1385a22.}}

1163:
** δεῖ**:
_subjunctive_ δέη, sometimes _contraction_ δῆ (in codices of Comedy texts, as {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 265)}}; ὁπόσου κα δῆ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 4.1484.65 (Epid.), compare “SIG” 245 G 47 (Delph.); _optative_ δέοι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.4}}; _infinitive_ δεῖ; _participle_ δέον (see. infr. IV): _imperfect_ ἔδει, _Ionic dialect_ ἔδεε: _future_ δεήσει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 941, etc.}}: _aorist_ I ἐδέησε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.77, etc.}} —Impers. from δέω (A), **there is need** (the sense of moral obligation, properly belonging to χρή, is later, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 583, etc.):}}
**I** c._accusative_ _person_ et _infinitive_, **it is needful for** one to do, **one must,** once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, τί δὲ δεῖ πολεμιζέμεναι.. Ἀργείους}} why **need** the Argives fight? {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.337; δ. [μ᾽] ἐλθεῖν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 6.28, etc.}}: with _nominative_ of the _pronoun_, ἡγούμην.. δεῖν.. μεγαλοψυχότερος φαίνεσθαι [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.235: rarely δεῖ σ᾽ ὅπως δείξεις, ={δεῖ σε δεῖξαι}, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 556, compare “Ph.” 54; δεῖ σ᾽ ὅπως μηδὲν διοίσεις 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 108; (the full _construction_ in 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 77 τοῦτο δεῖ σοφισθῆναι.. ὅπως γενήσῃ)}}]: rarely with _dative_ _person_, **there is need of..** for.., θεοῖσι προσβαλεῖν χθονὶ ἄλλην δεήσει γαῖαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 941, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.4.35, “Oec.” 7.20: the _accusative_ _person_ is often omitted, ἐκ τῶν μανθάνειν δεῖ (i.e. ἡμᾶς) 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.8, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 567, “Eu.” 826, etc.}}
**I.2** with _accusative_ _of things_ et _infinitive_, δεῖ τι γενέσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.26; παραδείγματα, καθ᾽ ἃ δέοι ἀποκρίνεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 79a, etc.}}; also ἐπεὶ δέ οἱ ἔδεε κακῶς γενέσθαι since **it was fated** for him.., since he **was doomed..,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.161, compare 8.53, 9.109, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 825}}; for οἴομαι δεῖν, see at {οἴομαι}.
**I.3** _absolutely_ with _infinitive_ understood, μὴ πεῖθ᾽ ἃ μὴ δεῖ (i.e. πείθειν) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1442, compare “OT” 1273}}; εἴ τι δέοι, ἤν τι δέῃ (i.e. γενέσθαι), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.59, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.44}}; κἂν δέῃ (i.e. τροχάζειν), τροχάζῶ [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|{5-6th c.BC}: Philetaerus Comicus 3.}}]
**II** with _genitive_ _of things_, **there is need of,** frequently with _negative_, οὐδὲν ἂν δέοι πολλοῦ ἀργυρίου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 45a, etc.; τί δεῖ τῆς ἀρετῆ; 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1309b10}}; sometimes with _infinitive_ added, μακροῦ λόγου δεῖ ταῦτ᾽ ἐπεξελθεῖν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 870, compare 875, “Supp.” 407.}}
**II.b** frequently in phrases, πολλοῦ δεῖ **there wants much, far from it,** ὀλίγου δεῖ **there wants little, all but;** in full with _infinitive_, πολλοῦ δεῖ οὕτως ἔχειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 35d; τοὺς Πλαταιέας ἐλαχίστου ἐδέησε διαφθεῖραι [τὸ πῦρ] 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.77; πολλοῦ γε δεῖ, πολλοῦ γε καὶ δεῖ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 543, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.300, 21.71}}; τοῦ πλεῦνος αἰεὶ ἔδεε **there was** always further **to travel,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.43; τοῦ παντὸς δεῖ 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Merc.Cond.” 13: also ὀλίγου δεῖν}} _absolutely_, in same sense, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 22a, etc.; μικροῦ δεῖν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.29.}}
**II.2** with _dative_ _person_ added, δεῖ μοί τινος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 848, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 565, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.71, etc.}}
**II.3** with _accusative_ _person_ added, αὐτὸν γάρ σε δεῖ προμηθέως {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 86, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 837, “Hipp.” 23.}}
**II.4** rarely with Subj. in _nominative_, δεῖ μοί τι something **is needful** to me, ἓν δεῖ μόνον μοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 594; εἴ τι δέοι τῷ χορῷ 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.12; πρῶτον μὲν τοῦτο δεῖ, ὑπειληφέναι.. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 10.15.}}
**III** _middle_, δεῖται **there is need,** with _genitive_, δεῖταί σοι τῆς αὐτῆς ἐρωτήσεως {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 79c; ὅσων δέοιτο 4th c.BC: Aeneas Tacticus 13.1; ἑτέρου δεῖσθαι στρατηγοῦ νομίζων 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pelopidas” 26}}: with _infinitive_, ὥστε βραχἔ ἐμοὶ δεῖσθαι φράσαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 570 (codices); δεήσεται.. ἀναγράφεσθαι 4th c.BC: Aeneas Tacticus 31.9; τήν μευ γλῶσσαν ἐκτεμεῖν δεῖται 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 6.41.}}
**IV** _neuter_ _participle_ **δέον **(**δεῖν** is uncertain in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 220, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 14.7, compare 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Adverbiis;” 132.30, Hdn.Gr. 2.328, +others, 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus)}}: _absolutely_, **it being needful** or **fitting,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 355d, etc.}}; οὐκ ἀπήντα, δέον, he did not appear in court, **though he ought to have done so,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.90}}: with _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 988}}; οὐδὲν δέον **there being** no **need,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.65, etc.}}: _future_ ὡς αὐτίκα δεῆσον διώκειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.2.8}}: _aorist_ δεῆσαν [{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Fabius Maximus” 9, etc.: also δέον ἂν εἴη, ={δέοι ἄν}, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.37.5, etc.: less frequently _genitive_ _absolutely_ δέοντος}}] with _accusative_ _infinitive_, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Cornutus Philosophus “de Natura Deorum” 17.}}
**IV.2** _substantive_ **δέον**, τό (see entry _vocative_).

1164:
** δεῖγμα**, ατος, τό,
(δείκνυμι) **sample, pattern,** καρπῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 15.54, compare “POxy.” 113.5 (2nd c.AD)}}; τοῦ βίου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 988}}; λαβὼν δ᾽ Ἄδραστον δεῖγμα τῶν ἐμῶν λόγων taking him **as evidence** of.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 354; μὴ.. αὐτοὶ καθ᾽ ὑμῶν αὐτῶν δ. τοιοῦτον ἐξενέγκητε 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.183; τοῦτο τὸ δ. ἐξενηνοχὼς περὶ αὐτοῦ [prev. author] 19.12, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 788c}}; δείγματος εἵνεκα by way **of sample,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.65; δείγματος χάριν 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 11.40}}; δ. προφέρειν, ἐκτίθεσθαι, παρασχεῖν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 718b, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.24.9, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “Ars Rhetorica” 6.5; δ. μικροψυχίας 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Georg.fragment” 3}}; δ. ψυχῆς **sign** of life, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Prom.Es” 2 (assuming variant); δ. φιλοσοφίας Ἀττικῆς [prev. author] “Scyth.” 7; [ἀνδριάντα] δ. ἀρετᾶς θεμένα “epigram Gr.” 860.6, compare “IG” 14.967, etc.}}
**b** **plan, sketch,** {{Refs|PGiss. 15.3.}}
**c** **sense-evidence,** τὰ ἐπὶ τοῦ ὀργάνου δείγματα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “De rerum natura - Wien. Stud.” 11.7.}}
**2** **mart, bazaar,** in the Piraeus, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.1.21, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator “Fragmenta” 75.6; περιεπάτουν ἐν τῷ δ. τῷ ἡμετέρῳ 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 35.29; elsewhere, “IPE” 1.16B49 (from Olbia), Aen. Tact.30.2, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.88.8, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 19.45.}}
**2.b** _metaphorically_, δ. δικῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 979.}}

1165:
** δειγμᾰτ-ίζω**,
**make a show of,** {{NT|NT.Col.2.15}}; **make an example of,** {{NT|NT.Matt.1.19}}; **furnish a sample,** in _passive_, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“PTeb.” 576 1st c.BC-1st c.AD}}; δειγματισθήσεται.. ἀπολέσθαι **will be proved to..,** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|PRyl. 1.28.32 (4th c.AD).}}
**2** **make trial of, test,** {{Refs|PHolm. 18.20, 22.29.}}
**II** _intransitive_, **appear,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Aristophanes Byzantinus Philosophus “Historiae Animalium Epitome subiunctis Aeliani Timothei aliorumque eclogis - pars” 41.19, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιστολαί” 19 (in “Hermes” 53.67).}}

1166:
** δείκνυμι**
(also **δεικνύω** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 451, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 562}}; _Ionic dialect_ **δέκνυμι** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“GDI” 5653b14 (ἀπο-, Chios), 5493b25 (ἀπο-, Milet.), frequently in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus}}; Cretan dialect **δίκνυμι** (προ-) {{Refs|“GDI” 5112)}}; _3rd.pers._ _singular_ δείκνυ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 526}}; _imperative_ δείκνυε{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 502, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 228e}}, _3rd.pers._ _singular_ δεικνύτω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1532}}: _imperfect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἐδείκνυσαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.5.33, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.213}}, also **-υον** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.150, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.76, etc.}}; _3rd.pers._ _singular_ δείκνυεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.220}}: _future_ δείξω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.25, etc.}}, _Ionic dialect_ δέξω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.179, +others:}}_aorist 1_ ἔδειξα, _Epic dialect_ δεῖξα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.174, etc.}}, _Ionic dialect_ ἔδεξα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.30, +others}}: _perfect_ δέδειχα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 268, (ἐπι-) 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 26.16, (ἀνα-) 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.48.3:}} —_middle_, with _perfect_ _passive_, for _Epic dialect_ forms δεικνύμενος, δείδεκτο, δειδέχαται, δειδέχατο, see at {δειδίσκομαι}:—_passive_, _future_ δειχθήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.1, 12.4; δεδείξομαι 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.416d, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 23.26, +others}}: _aorist_ ἐδείχθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 1209, etc., _Ionic dialect_ ἐδέχθην (ἀπ-) 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1 “Prooem.”}}: _perfect_ δέδειγμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 432}}, _Ionic dialect_ δέδεκται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ τέχνης” 10}}:—**bring to light, show forth,** [θεὸς] ἡμῖν δεῖξε [τέρας] {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.178; δεικνὺς σῆμα βροτοῖσιν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.244; ἄγος δ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1427}}; τὸν κτανόντα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 278; ἵν᾽ ἐλαίας.. ἔδειξε κλάδον Ἀθάνα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 802}}; of artists, **portray, represent,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Im.” 5}}; **cause,** δυσθέατα πήματα ἐδείξατ᾽ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 982 codices}}; **render** so and so, τυφλοὺς τοὺς ἐμβλέποντας δεικνύει {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 83; τινὰ ὑπὸ τῶν τραυμάτων δείξας νεκρόν 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 34.2.21:}} —_middle_, δείκνυμαι **set before one,** ἄεθλα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.701.}}
**2** **show, point out,** δ. Ἀλέξανδρον Μενελάῳ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.452}}; δέσμιον.. ἔδειξ᾽ Ἀχαιοῖς (i.e. αὐτόν) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 609, compare 492, 630}}; αὐτὸ δ. experiment ** will show,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 177, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 200e, “Hp.Ma.” 288b}}; δείξει δὴ τάχα alone, **time will show,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1261}}; δ. εἴς τινα **point** towards, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.150}}:—_middle_, δείξατο δ᾽ εἰς Κρονίωνα {{Refs|“hymnus ad Mercurium” 367.}}
**3** **show, make known,** especially by words, **explain,** ὁδόν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.25, etc.; ἀντολὰς ἐγὼ ἄστρων ἔδειξα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 458, compare 482.}}
**4** **show, prove,** with _participle_, ποῦ γὰρ ὢν δείξω φίλο; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 802; ἔδειξαν ἕτοιμοι ὄντες 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.73, compare 5.72; δεικνύω ἐσπουδακώς 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 562; δείξω αὐτὸν πολλῶν θανάτων ὄντ᾽ ἄξιον 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.21; εἰ.. δειχθήσεται τοῦτο πεποιηκώς [prev. work]160; ἱκανώτατα δέδεικται ψυχὴ τῶν πάντων πρεσβυτάτη 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 896b: followed by a _relative_ clause with ὡς.., ὅτι.., εἰ..,etc., 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 176, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.76, 143, etc.; πᾶσα ἀπόδειξίς τι κατά τινος δείκνυσι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Anthologia Graeca” 0.90b34: ὅπερ ἔδει δεῖξαι, = Q.E. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator, 3rd c.BC: Euclides Geometra 1.4, +others: _absolutely_, δέδεικται}} **it is clear** or **proven,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 66d, etc.; δεδείξεται 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus [prev. cited]}}
**5** of accusers, **inform against,** τινά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 278 (Dobree ex Scholia ἐγὼ ἐνδείκνυμι).}}
**6** **display, exhibit,** ἀγλαΐαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 6.46}}; ἀρετήν, προθυμίαν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.37, 6.11.}}
**7** **offer, proffer,** καὶ τὰ πίστ᾽ ἐδειξάτην {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 651}}. (Cf. Latin _dīco,_ Gothic _gateihan_ 'announce', OHG. **zeigōn** 'show'.)

1167:
** δειλία**, ἡ,
**timidity, cowardice,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.37, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 536, etc.}}; δειλίην ὀφλεῖν to be charged with **cowardice,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.26}}; δειλίας ὀφλεῖν (i.e. δίκην) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.74}}; ἔνοχος δειλίας (i.e. δίκῃ) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 1.45}}; opposed to ἀνδρεία, θρασύτης, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 648b, “Ti.” 87a.}}
**II** **misery,** {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Procopius Caesariensis Historicus “de Bello Gothico” 4.32.}}

1169:
** δειλός**, ή, όν,
(δέος):
**I** of persons, **cowardly,** opposed to ἄλκιμος, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.278}}; opposed to ἀνδρεῖος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 239a, etc.}}: hence, **vile, worthless,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.293; δειλαί τοι δειλῶν γε καὶ ἐγγύαι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.351}}; opposed to ἐσθλός, **lowborn, mean,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Fragmenta” 164; πλοῦτος καὶ δειλοῖσιν ἀνθρώπων ὁμιλεῖ 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 1.50; ἀγαθοὶ δειλῶν ἐπὶ δαῖτας ἴσιν 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 289}}; of animals, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.108: with _genitive_, δειλὸς μυάγρης}}**afraid of..,** {{Refs|Anthologia Graeca 9.410 (Tull. Sab.)}}: c._infinitive_ {{Refs 1st c.BC+|[prev. work]6.232 (1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Crinagoras Epigrammaticus)}}. _adverb_ **-λῶς**{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus “Adon.” 15, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.26b.}}
**I.2** more commonly, **miserable, wretched,** with a compassionate sense, δειλοὶ βροτοί **poor** mortals! {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.31, +others}}; ἆ δειλέ **poor wretch!** ἆ δειλοί **poor wretches!** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|17.201, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.351; ἆ δειλὲ ξείνων 14.361; Πατροκλῆος δειλοῖο 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.670.}}
**II** of things, **miserable, wretched,** γῆρας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 113}}; δ. δ᾽ ἐνὶ πυθμένι φειδώ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 369; τὰ δ. κέρδη 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 326}}; ἔργα, λόγος, etc., {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 307, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 757, etc.}}: _comparative_, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Longinus Rhetor 2.1}}: _superlative_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 123}}: _neuter_ _plural_ as _adverb_, ὀχλεῖ μοι δειλὰ ὁ Τρωΐλος {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“PIand.” 11.4 (3rd c.AD).}} —Trag. use δειλός chiefly in former sense, δείλαιος in latter.

1170:
** δεῖνα**, ὁ, ἡ, τό,
_genitive_ δεῖνος, _dative_ δεῖνι, _accusative_ δεῖνα: sometimes _indeclinable_ (see. below): _nominative_ **δεῖν**, ὁ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 58: _genitive_ and _dative_ τοῦ δείνατος, τῷ δείνατι, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 60.12, “Etymologicum Magnum” 614.51}}:— **such an one, so-and-so,** always with _Article_, ὁ δεῖνα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 918, etc.; τὸν δεῖνα τὸν τοῦ δεῖνα [prev. author] “Th.” 622; ὁ δεῖνα τοῦ δεῖνος τὸν δεῖν᾽ εἰσήγγειλεν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 13.5; ἃ ἂν ὁ δ. ἢ ὁ δ. εἴπῃ [prev. author] 2.31; ὁ δ. καὶ ὁ δ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1416a23; ἡ δεῖνα 2nd c.AD(?): Antoninus Liberalis Mythographus 22}}; τὸ δ., euphemistic for τὸ πέος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 1149, cf. Scholia 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Bis Acc.” 23; τὸ δ. δ᾽ ἐσθίει;}} do you eat **such a fish?** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 129.6: in _genitive_, ἐμὸς ἢ τοῦ δεῖνος}} mine or **some other's,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1262a3: _dative_, τῷ δεῖνι μεμφόμενος 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.104, compare 37.56: _plural_, οἱ δεῖνες [prev. author] 24.180; τῶν δείνων [prev. author] 20.106.}}
**II** τὸ δ. in Comedy texts as an interjection to express an idea which suddenly strikes one, **by the way, mark you,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 524, “Pax” 268, etc.: in later Prose, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Vit.Auct.” 19.}}

1171:
Included with: ** δεινός**, ή, όν,
(δέος,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 198b)}} **fearful, terrible;** in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, of persons and things, Χάρυβδις {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.260; κλαγγή 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.49; ὅπλα 10.254: frequently in _neuter_, δεινὸν ἀῧσαι 11.10; βροντᾶν 20.56; δεινὸν δέρκεσθαι 3.342; παπταίνειν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.608; δεινὰ δ᾽ ὑποδρὰ ἰδών 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.13}}; δ. ἰδέσθαι **fearful** to behold, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.405; δ. μὲν ὁρᾶν, δ. δὲ κλύειν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 141; εἰ καὶ δεινόν τῳ ἀκοῦσαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.122; δεινὴ παρὰ τοῖς εἰδόσιν ἡ βάσανος 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.30}}; in milder sense, **awful,** δεινή τε καὶ αἰδοίη θεός {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.394, compare 3.172, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.22, etc.; τὸ δεινόν}}**danger, suffering, horror,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 634, etc.}}; **awe, terror,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Eu.” 517; ὅπου τὸ δ. ἐλπὶς οὐδὲν ὠφελεῖ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 196; πρὸς τὸ δ. ἔρχεσθαι [prev. work] 351: in _plural_, ἐκτὸς ὄντα πημάτων τὰ δείν᾽ ὁρᾶν [prev. author] “Ph.” 504; εἰ δείν᾽ ἔδρασας, δεινὰ καὶ παθεῖν σε δεῖ [prev. author] “fragment” 962, etc.}}; δεινὸν γίγνεται μή.. there is **danger** that.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.157}}; οὐδὲν δεινοὶ ἔσονται μὴ ἀποστέωσιν no **fear** of their revolting, {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.155, etc.}}; δεινότατον μή.. the **greatest danger** lest.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 3.1}}; δεινόν ἐστι, with _infinitive_, it is **dangerous** to do, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.87}}; δεινὸν ποιεῖσθαι **take ill, complain of, be indignant at** a thing: _absolutely_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.102, etc.}}: with _infinitive_, ὑπὸ Μήδων ἄρχεσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.127, etc.; also δεινὰ ποιεῖν}}**make complaints,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 3.14, 5.41; ἐν δεινῷ τίθεσθαι 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 18.9.8; δεινόν τι ἔσχε αὐτὸν ἀτιμάζεσθαι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.61}}; δεινὸν or δεινὰ παθεῖν suffer **illegal, arbitrary** treatment, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 252, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 317b, etc.; δεινότερα π. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.13; τὸ δ. τὸ πείσομαι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.11: in Oratt., δεινὸν ἂν εἴη εἰ.. 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.30, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.88, etc.}} _adverb_ -νῶς, φέρειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.121}}. γ; δ. καὶ ἀπόρως ἔχει μοι I am in **dire** straits, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.1; δ. ἔχειν τῇ ἐνδείᾳ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.4.23; δ. διατεθῆναι τυπτόμενος 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 3.27.}}
**II** **marvellously strong, powerful**: δ. σάκος the **mighty** shield, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.245}}; simply, **wondrous, marvellous, strange,** τὸ συγγενές τοι δεινὸν ἥ θ᾽ ὁμιλία kin and social ties **have strange power,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 39; δ. τὸ κοινὸν σπλάγχνον [prev. author] “Th.” 1036; δ. τὸ τίκτειν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 770; πολλὰ τὰ δ. κοὐδὲν ἀνθρώπου -ότερον πέλει. [prev. author] “Ant.” 333}}; δ. ἵμερος, ἔρως, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.3, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 169c; οἶκτος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 298, etc.; δ. λέγεις πρᾶγμα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 298c; δ. γ᾽ εἶπας, εἰ καὶ ζῇς θανών 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1127}}; frequently δεινὸν ἂν εἴη εἰ.. it were **strange** that.., as {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 592.}} _adverb_ -νῶς **marvellously, exceedingly,** δ. μέλαινα, ἄνυδρος, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.76, 149; δ. ἐν φυλακῇσι εἶναι [prev. author] 3.152; δ. πώς εἰμ᾽ ἐπιλήσμων 5th c.BC: Metagenes Comicus 2, etc.: _comparative_ -οτέρως Scholia Min. 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.97.}}
**III** **clever, skilful,** first in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.23}} ἀνὴρ δ. τε καὶ σοφό; of Odysseus, γλώσσῃ.. δεινοῦ καὶ σοφοῦ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 440, compare “OC” 806, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.2.3, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 7.12; σοφὸς καὶ δ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 341a}}; opposed to σοφός, of **practical ability,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Phdr.” 245c, “Tht.” 164d}}; opposed to ἰδιώτης, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.35}}: with _infinitive_, δεινὸς εὑρεῖν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 59; δεινοὶ πλέκειν τοι μηχανὰς Αἰγύπτιοι [prev. author] “fragment” 373}}; δ. λέγειν **clever** at speaking, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 545, etc.}}; δ. εἰπεῖν is rare, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.150; νόσος δ. φαγεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 243; δ. πράγμασι χρῆσθαι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.3}}; αἱ εὐπραξίαι δ. συγκρύψαι τὰ ὀνείδη are **wonderfully liable** to.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 2.20: with _accusative_, δ. τὴν τέχνην 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 364; δ. περὶ τοὺς λόγους τοὺς εἰς τὰ δικαστήρια 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 304d; ἐς τὰ πάντα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 968}}; δ. περὶ τὸ ἀδικεῖν, περὶ Ὁμήρου, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 405c, “Ion” 531a; δ. ἀμφί τι 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Tactica” 9.5; δ. κατὰ χειρουργίαν 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 3.1; ἐν λόγοισι δ. Ὑπερείδης 4th c.BC: Timocles Comicus 4.7}} (but also of the **forcible, vehement,** style in oratory, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 240, +others)}}; in bad sense, **over-clever,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 3c; δ. ὑπὸ πανουργίας [prev. author] “Tht.” 176d, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1144a27. (For δϝεινός, compare Δϝενία, _genitive_ of _proper name_ Δεινίας, “IG” 4.858.)}}

1172:
** δειπν-έω**,
_future_ **-ήσω**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 1084, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.3.35}}, **-ήσομαι** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 11.9, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 11.6:}}_aorist_ ἐδείπνησα, _Epic dialect_ δείπνησα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.111, etc.}}: _perfect_ δεδείπνηκα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 1133, etc.}}; _Attic dialect_ _1st pers._ _plural_ δεδείπναμεν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 109, 4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 91}}; _infinitive_ δεδειπνάναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 464, 249, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Comicus 144}}: _pluperfect_ ἐδεδειπνήκεσαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.18}}; _Epic dialect_ δεδειπνήκειν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.359}}:—**make a meal,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus (see. δεῖπνον)}}: in _Attic dialect_ always, **take the chief meal, dine,** once in Trag., δειπνεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 894 (uncertain reading)}}; δ. τὸ ἄριστον **make** breakfast **serve as dinner,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.2.11}}; δ. παρά τινι **with** one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.18}}; [ἐν πρυτανείῳ] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.45.}}
**2** with _accusative_, δ. ἄρτον **make a meal on** bread, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 442; δ. μοσχίον 4th c.BC: Ephippus Comicus 15.13; κοτύλην μίαν 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 221.17; ξίφη 4th c.BC: Mnesimachus Comicus 7}}; δ. τἀλλότρια, of parasites, frequently in Comedy texts,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|Theopom p.34, 4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 72, etc.; also δ. ἀπό τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 890.}}
**II** _active_, **entertain,** τινάς {{Refs|“Milet.” 7.68, “Inscription Cos” 131.}}

1173:
** δεῖπνον**, τό,
**meal**: in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} sometimes **noonday meal,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.86}}; sometimes ={ἄριστον}, **morning meal,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|2.381, 10.578, 19.171s q., 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.94s q., 500}}; sometimes ={δόρπον}, **evening meal,** {{Refs|17.176, 20.390s q}}.; later,**the midday meal,** σῖτον εἰδέναι διώρισα, ἄριστα, δεῖπνα, δόρπα θ᾽ αἱρεῖσθαι τρίτα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 182}}; later, **the afternoon meal, dinner** or **supper,** σοὶ δὲ μελήσει, ὅτανᾖ δεκάπουν τὸ στοιχεῖον, λιπαρῷ χωρεῖν ἐπὶ δ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 652: frequently in _plural_, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 779, “El.” 203 (Lyric poetry); δ. Θυέστου 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1008 (Lyric poetry)}}; ἀπὸ δείπνου straight-way after **the meal,** ἀπὸ δ. αὐτοῦ θωρήσσοντο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.54, compare 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.17}}; καλεῖν ἐπὶ δεῖπνον, κεκλῆσθαι ἐπὶ δ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 72, 119.2; δ. παρασκευάζειν 5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 45, 172; παραθεῖναι [prev. author] 184; ποιεῖν 4th c.BC: Dionysius Comicus 2.4}}; of animals, etc., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus “Epigrammata” 11, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 1.12, 12.27.}}
**2** generally, **food, provender,** ἵπποισιν δεῖπνον δότε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.383; ὄρνισι δεῖπνον 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 801}}; κοράκεσσιν epigram cited in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Heroicus” 19.17.}}

1174:
** δεισῐδαίμ-ων**, ον,
_genitive_ ονος, (δείδω) **fearing the gods,**
**1** in good sense, **pious, religious,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.3.58, “Ages.” 11.8; δ. εἶναι καὶ φροντίζειν τῶν θεῶν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1315a1; φίλος θνητοῖς εἴς τ᾽ ἀθανάτους δ. “IG” 14.1683}}: _comparative_ **-έστερος**{{NT|NT.Act.17.22.}}
**2** in bad sense, **superstitious,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 16, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “de Pietate - Herkulanische Studien” 105}}; δ. διάθεσις, ={δεισιδαιμονία}, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.62}}: _comparative_ **-έστερος**{{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 2.132}}: _superlative_ **-έστατος**{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Pr.Im.” 27.}} _adverb_ -όνως{{Refs 1st c.AD+|Aristeas 129, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.195, 1st c.AD: Cornutus Philosophus “de Natura Deorum” 27.}}

1175:
** δεισῐδαιμ-ονία**, ἡ,
**fear of the gods, religious feeling,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.56.7, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Herc.” 1251.10, “CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 2737b11 (from Aphrodisias), 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.70, etc.; ἡ τῶν θεῶν δ. [prev. author] 11.89.}}
**2** in bad sense, **superstition,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 16, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 12.24.5; ἡ πρὸς τὰ ζῷα δ. 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.83}}; περὶ Δεισιδαιμονίας, title of work by {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus}}

1181:
** δέκᾰτος**
{{Refs 4th c.AD+|(4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus}} **δέκοτος** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 5(2).282 (Mantinea, 5th c.BC)}}, also _Aeolic dialect_ in {{Refs|“epigram Gr.” 988.5 (from Balbilla))}}, η, ον: (δέκα):— **tenth,** Ἠώς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.175, etc.}}; as a round number, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.18, etc.}}
**II** **δεκάτη** (i.e. μερίς), ἡ, **tenth part, tithe,** τᾶς δεκάτας δεκάταν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 141.4, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.135, etc.; τῇ θεῷ τὰς δ. ἐξαιρεθῆναι 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 20.24}}; τὰ ἐκ τῆς δ. the produce **of the tenth,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 12.91, compare “Tab.Defix.” 99.14: especially as a customs-duty, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.60; δεκάτη μόσχων “PTeb.” 307.8 (3rd c.AD).}}
**II.2** δεκάτη (i.e. ἡμέρα), ἡ, **the tenth day,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.83, +others}}
**II.2.b** δ. προτέρα· ἡ πρὸ εἰκάδος, ὡς ὑστέρα· ἡ μετ᾽ εἰκάδα, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}
**II.3** **festival on the tenth day after birth,** when the child has a name given it, τὴν δ. θύειν to give a **naming-day** feast, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 922, compare 494; δ. ὑπέρ τινος ἑστιᾶσαι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 40.28, compare 39.22.}}
**II.4** δεκάτα· τάξις, ἄθροισμα, καὶ ἡ τῶν εἴκοσιν ἁρμάτων τάξις, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}
**II.5** δέκατον, τό, **tenth part,** {{LXX|LXX.Lev.23.13, 17 (_plural_).}}

1182:
** δέκᾰτος**
{{Refs 4th c.AD+|(4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus}} **δέκοτος** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 5(2).282 (Mantinea, 5th c.BC)}}, also _Aeolic dialect_ in {{Refs|“epigram Gr.” 988.5 (from Balbilla))}}, η, ον: (δέκα):— **tenth,** Ἠώς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.175, etc.}}; as a round number, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.18, etc.}}
**II** **δεκάτη** (i.e. μερίς), ἡ, **tenth part, tithe,** τᾶς δεκάτας δεκάταν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 141.4, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.135, etc.; τῇ θεῷ τὰς δ. ἐξαιρεθῆναι 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 20.24}}; τὰ ἐκ τῆς δ. the produce **of the tenth,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 12.91, compare “Tab.Defix.” 99.14: especially as a customs-duty, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.60; δεκάτη μόσχων “PTeb.” 307.8 (3rd c.AD).}}
**II.2** δεκάτη (i.e. ἡμέρα), ἡ, **the tenth day,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.83, +others}}
**II.2.b** δ. προτέρα· ἡ πρὸ εἰκάδος, ὡς ὑστέρα· ἡ μετ᾽ εἰκάδα, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}
**II.3** **festival on the tenth day after birth,** when the child has a name given it, τὴν δ. θύειν to give a **naming-day** feast, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 922, compare 494; δ. ὑπέρ τινος ἑστιᾶσαι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 40.28, compare 39.22.}}
**II.4** δεκάτα· τάξις, ἄθροισμα, καὶ ἡ τῶν εἴκοσιν ἁρμάτων τάξις, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}
**II.5** δέκατον, τό, **tenth part,** {{LXX|LXX.Lev.23.13, 17 (_plural_).}}

1184:
** δεκ-τός**, ή, όν,
**to be received** or **accepled, acceptable,** {{LXX+NT|LXX.Isa.61.2, +others, NT.Luke.4.24}}; δεκτόν [ἐστι] it is **an accepted principle,** with _infinitive_, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Erotianus Grammaticus “Praef.”}}
**2** **to be grasped,** χείρ {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “Protrepticus” 21.ιθ.}}
**II** **to be taken, understood,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 2.269 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}

1185:
** δελε-άζω**,
(δέλεαρ) **entice** or **catch by a bait,** τὴν γραῦν δ. λεπαστῇ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 45, compare 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 2.15.3; δ. τινὰς ἐπὶ πλεονεξίαν 1st c.AD: Onosander (Onasander) Tacticus 6.10}}:— _passive_, γαστρὶ δελεάζεσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.1.4, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 8.34, 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Sent. Vat.” 16, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ παρρησίας” p.14O.; ῥᾳστώνῃ καὶ σχολῇ 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.45}}; ὑπὸ χρημάτων, ὑπὸ τῆς ἡδονῆς, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Apol.” 9, 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 6.185a.}}
**II** with _accusative_ _cognate_, νῶτον ὑὸς περὶ ἄγκιστρον δ. **put** it on the hook **as a bait,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.70}}; but δ. ἄγκιστρον ἰσχάδι **bait** it with a fig, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Pisc.” 47; δ. ἄγκιστρον ἐπ᾽ ἄλλους to catch others. [prev. work] 48.}}

1188:
** δεξιός**, ά, όν,
**on the right hand** or **side,** opposed to ἀριστερός, δ. μαζός, γλουτός, ὦμος, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.481, 5.66, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.462}}; τὸ δ. (i.e. κέρας) **the right** of an army, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 2.9, etc.: frequently in adverbial usages, ἐπὶ δεξιά}} on or to **the right,** νωμῆσαι βῶν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.238, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.51; ἐν τῷ ἐπὶ δ. τοῦ ὀπισθοδόμου “IG” 12.92.55}}; ἐπὶ δεξιόφιν (_Epic dialect_ _genitive_) towards **the right,** {{NT+8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.308; χειρὸς εἰς τὰ δ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 598, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 943b29; ἀπὸ τῶν δ. [prev. author] “Cael.” 284b28; ἐν τοῖς δ. NT.Mark.16.5; ἐκ δεξιῶν 2nd-1st c.BC(?): Hero Mechanicus “Automatopoetica” 26.4, NT.Matt.20.21, +others; ἐπὶ δ. χειρός 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 25.18; κατὰ δ. χειρός 4th-3rd c.BC: Aratus Epicus 707; πρὸς δ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.69; εἰς δεξιά 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 43b}}; δεξιά as _adverb_, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.82.9 (assuming variant).}}
**II** **fortunate,** especially of the flight of birds and other omens, δ. ὄρνις {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.821, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.154, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 489, “Michel” 727 (from Ephesus); ἀοιδὰν.. δεξιωτάταν ὀπαδόν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 3.8; βροντή 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.1.3}}. _adverb_ **-ῶς**{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 3.9.12.}}
**III** Astron., **northerly,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Cleomedes Astronomus 1.1.}}
**IV** _metaphorically_, **dexterous, ready, skilful, clever,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 5(4).61, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 428, 834, +others, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.82, etc.; δεξιὸν ποιεῖν}}**a clever thing,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.19; Εὐριπίδου δρᾶμα δεξιώτατον 5th c.BC: Strattis Comicus 1; δ. περὶ τὰς δίκας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hipparchus” 225c}}. _adverb_ **-ιῶς**{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 229.2; δ. ἔχειν πρός τι 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.660a; δ. φέρειν}}**in the right spirit,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ παρρησίας” pp.18,41}} O.: _superlative_ δεξιώτατα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 148.}}
**V** **courteous, kindly,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Nec.” 13, “Alex.” 57; τὸ δ. καὶ ἐπιεικές 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 14.296.}} _adverb_ -ιῶς{{Refs|[prev. work] 211}}. (Cf. Sanskrit _dák[snull]iṇas,_ Latin _dexter,_ etc.)

1189:
** δέω**,
**B** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1006, etc.}}: _future_ δεήσω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 395e}}: _aorist_ ἐδέησα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 30.8, _Epic dialect_ δῆσα only 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.100}}: _perfect_ δεδέηκα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 277d}}:—_middle_, _future_ δεήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.32, etc.}}, _Doric dialect_ δεοῦμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 120}}; later **-ηθήσομαι** {{LXX+1st c.AD+|LXX.Job.5.8, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.213c, etc.}}: _aorist_ ἐδεήθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.84, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 986, etc.}}: _perfect_ δεδέημαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.7.14, 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 8.22 (the forms δεήσω, etc., compared with the _Epic dialect_ ἐδεύησα, δεύομαι, point to root δεϝ)}}:—**lack, miss, stand in need of,** with _genitive_, ἐμεῖο δὲ δῆσε.. ἀλκτῆρα γενέσθαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” [prev. cited] (elsewhere 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus uses δεύω, which see)}}; παραδείγματος τὸ παράδειγμα αὐτὸ δεδέηκεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 277d, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.2.10.}}
**B.2** frequently in _Attic dialect_, πολλοῦ δέω I **want** much, i.e. **am** far **from,** mostly with _infinitive_ _present_, πολλοῦ δ. ἀπολογεῖσθαι I **am** far **from** defending myself, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 30d; πολλοῦ δεῖς εἰπεῖν [prev. author] “Men.” 79b; π. δ. ἀγνοεῖν [prev. author] “Ly.” 204e; π. γε δέουσι μαίνεσθαι [prev. author] “Men.” 92a; also μικροῦ ἔδεον ἐν χερσὶν εἶναι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.6.11, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Γεωργός” 25; τοσούτου δέω ἱκανὸς εἶναι λέγειν ὥστε.. 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 17.1; τοσούτου δέουσι μιμεῖσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 14.17 (also τοσοῦτον δέω εἰδέναι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 71a)}}; παρὰ μικρὸν ἐδέησα ἀποθανεῖν variant in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 17.42; simply ἐδέησα κινδύνῳ περιπεσεῖν 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 3.5: _absolutely_, πολλοῦ γε δέω}} I **am** far **from** it, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 228a; τοῦ παντὸς δέω 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1006; παντὸς δεῖ τοιοῦτος εἶναι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 221d (_impersonal_ πολλοῦ δεῖ, etc., see at {δεῖ} II.1. b)}}]: in _participle_, παλαστῆς δεόντων τεττάρων ποδῶν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 12.373.8; μικροῦ δέοντα τέτταρα τάλαντα 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.35}}; the _participle_ is frequently used to express numerals compounded with 8 or 9, ἀνδράσιν ἑνὸς δέουσι τριάκοντα {{Refs|“IG” 12.374.413}}; δυοῖν δέοντα τεσσεράκοντα forty **lacking** two, thirty-eight, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.14; πεντήκοντα δυοῖν δέοντα ἔτη 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.2}}; ἑνὸς δέον εἰκοστὸν ἔτος the {{Refs|20th year}}**save** one,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|the 19th, [prev. author] 8.6; δυοῖν δεούσαις εἴκοσι ναυσίν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.1.5: later, the}}_infinitive_ stands _absolutely_, περὶ τὰ ἑνὸς δεῖν πεντήκοντα fifty **save** one, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1390b11}}: _participle_ in _genitive_, τροφαλίδες μιᾶς δεούσης εἴκοσιν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|[prev. author] “HA” 522a31; πόλεων δυοῖν δεούσαιν ἑξήκοντα 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 5.27; ἑξήκοντα ἑνὸς δέοντος ἔτη 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pompeius” 79.}}
**B.3** _participle_ δέων, δέουσα, as _adjective_, **fit, proper,** ὁ καιρὸς οὐκ ἔστι χρόνος δέων {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Priora” 48b36; τοῖς δέουσι χρόνοις “IG” 12(3).247.11 (from Anaphe)}}; ἡ δέουσα ἑκάστων χρῆσις Hierocl.p.61 [{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus, etc.: especiallyfrequently in _neuter_, see at {δέον}.}}]
**B.4** δεῖ _impersonal_, see entry
**B.II** _deponent verb_ **δέομαι**: _contraction_ δῆσθε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 46}}, _participle_ δεύμενος {{Refs|[prev. author] 36}}: _future_ δεήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 53b:}}_aorist_ ἐδεήθην: always personal, and used by {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus only in form δεύομαι (see. δεύω B):}}
**B.II.1** _absolutely_, **to be in want** or **need, require,** mostly in _participle_, κάρτα δεόμενος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.59}}; οἱ δεόμενοι the **needy,** opposed to οἱ κεκτημένοι τὰς οὐσίας, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 6.67.}}
**B.II.1.b** **stand in need of, want,** with _genitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.36, etc.; τὰ σὰ δεῖται κολαστοῦ.. ἔπη 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1148; ῥώμης τινὸς δ. [prev. work] 1293}}; οὐδὲν δεῖσθαι τροφῆς **have** no **need of..,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.43}}; ἤν τι δέωνται βασιλέως if **they have** any **need** of him, {{Refs|[prev. work] 37: with}}_infinitive_, τοῦτο ἔτι δέομαι μαθεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 392d, compare “Euthd.” 275d, etc.}}; τὰ πράττεσθαι δεόμενα **things needing** to be done, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.3.3; τὰ δεόμενα}}**necessaries,** {{Refs|Inscriptiones Graecae 2.573.4}}; ἐπισκευάσαι τὰ δεόμενα parts **needing** repair, {{Refs|[prev. work]22.1176.15}}; τὸ δεόμενον **the point threatened,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 15.15.7}}; δεῖται _impersonal_, see at {δεῖ}.
**B.II.2** **beg** a thing **from** a person, with _double_ _genitive_ _of things_ et _person_, τῶν ἐδέετο σφέων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.157, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.32, etc.; μή μου δεηθῇς. ΘΗ. πράγματος ποίο; 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1170: frequently with _neuter_ _pronoun_ in _accusative_, τοῦτο ὑμῶν δέομαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 17c, compare “Smp.” 173e, etc.: with _accusative_ _cognate_, δέημα, or oftener δέησιν, δεῖσθαί τινος, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 1059, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.43, etc.: also with _accusative_ _of things_ only, ξύμφορα δ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.32; δυνατά τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 335e; δίκαια καὶ μέτρια ὑμῶν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 38.2; διαπράξωμαι ἃ δέομαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.3.29: with _genitive_ _person_ only, δεηθεὶς ὑμῶν}} **having begged a favour** of you, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.108}}: c._genitive_ _person_ et _infinitive_, ἐδέετο τοῦ δήμου φυλακῆς πρὸς αὐτοῦ κυρῆσαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.59, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 336a, etc.; δ. τινὸς ὥστε.. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.119; ὅπως.. 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Antonius” 84: rarely with _accusative_ _person_, ἐδέοντο Βοιωτοὺς ὅπως παραδώσουσι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.36: parenthetic, δέομαι}}**I pray,** {{LXX|LXX.Gen.44.18.}}

1192:
** δέρμα**, ατος, τό,
(δέρω) **skin, hide,** συός {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.548, +others; κριοῦ 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4 161; δ. αἴγειον “PEdgar” 11.8 (3rd c.BC), etc.}}; λέοντος a lion's **skin** for a cloak, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.23; κελαινόν, of a shield, 6.117}}; of **skins prepared for bags, bottles,** etc., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.291}}; of **a man's skin,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.341, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.431, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 98d, etc.}}; of **a man's skin** stripped off, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.64, 5.25; παλαιὸν δ. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 275.4; περὶ τῷ δ. δέδοικα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 27, compare “Pax” 746; ἀνὴρ κατὰ δέρμα θαυμαστὸς οἷος 2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor “Orationes” 51(27).38}}; of **the shell** of a tortoise, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 429, 1292.}}
**2** **skin** of fruits, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 4.14.10}}; περικαρπίων δέρματα **outer coverings** of seed-vessels, {{Refs|[prev. work] 1.2.6.}}**wallet, scrip,** {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}

1194:
** δέρω**
{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 485, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 285c, etc.}}:—also **δείρω** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.39, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 442, “Av.” 365, 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 361}}: _imperfect_ ἔδερον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.167}}, _Epic dialect_ δέρον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.61}}: _future_ δερῶ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 370}}: _aorist_ ἔδειρα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.422, (ἀπ-) 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.25, (ἐκ-) 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 616a:}} —_middle_, see. {ἀναδέρω}:—_passive_, _future_ δᾰρήσομαι {{NT+2nd c.AD+|NT.Mark.13.9, “POxy.” 653b (2nd c.AD):}}_aorist_ ἐδάρην [ᾰ] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Μονόστιχοι - Fragmenta Comicorum Graecorum” 422, (ἀπ-) 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.5.9, (ἐκ-) 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.26}}; _participle_ δαρθείς {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Nicochares Comicus 8:}}_perfect_ δέδαρμαι (see. below):— **skin, flay,** of animals, δ. βοῦς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.167: proverbial, κύνα δ. δεδαρμένην}} 'flog a dead horse', {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 179}}; ἀσκὸς δεδάρθαι **to have one's** skin **flayed off,** {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus 33.7}}; δερῶ σε θύλακον κλοπῆς I **will make** a thief's purse **of** your **skin,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 370: proverbial, πρὶν ἐσφάχθαι δέρεις}} 'first catch your hare, then cook it', {{Refs|12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 1792.45}}; ἀέρα δέρειν 'plough the sands', {{Refs|[prev. author] 1215.50, 10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus}}
**2** Anatomy texts, **separate by avulsion,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herophilus Medicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 2.349.}}
**II** colloquially, **cudgel, thrash,** δέδοκταί μοι δέρεσθαι καὶ δέρειν δι᾽ ἡμέρας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 485, compare “Nu.” 442, “POxy.” [prev. cited] (2nd c.AD, _passive_): proverbial, ὁ μὴ δαρεὶς ἄνθρωπος οὐ παιδεύεται}} 'spare the rod and spoil the child', {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus [same place], compare “SIG” 1109.91 (2nd c.AD)}}: _metaphorically_, εἰς πρόσωπόν τινα δ. {{NT|NT.2Cor.11.20}}. (Cf. Lithuanian _derù_ 'flay', Sanskrit _dṛṇā´ti_ 'split'.)

1195:
** δεσμ-εύω**,
**fetter, put in chains,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|hymnus ad Bacchum 17, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 616, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 808e}}; **tie together,** as corn in the sheaf, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 481; δ. ἀγκάλας “PLond.” 1.131r426 (2nd c.AD)}}; χόρτον {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“PFlor.” 322.31 (3rd c.AD)}}; δ. ἔκ τινος **bind fast to..,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.93.4, 2nd c.AD: Apollodorus Damascenus Mechanicus 2.1.3.}}
**II** **lay snares for,** {{LXX|LXX.1Ki.24.12.}}

1197:
** δέσμ-η**, ἡ,
**package, bundle,** Test. cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 35.34, 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 117, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Fragmenta” 140, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 3.61.}}
**2** a **measure** in Egypt, φοινίκων {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PFay.” 119.4, +others; ἀσπαράγου “POxy.” 1212.4 (2nd c.AD).}}
**2.b** in Medicine, **handful,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|1st c.AD: Andromachus Poeta Medicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 13.1033; ὑσσώπου κόμης 2nd c.BC: Ezekiel Poeta Judaeus “Exagoge - Mnemosyne” 185.}}

1198:
** δέσμ-ιος**, ον,
**binding**: _metaphorically_, **binding as with a spell, enchaining,** with _genitive_, ὕμνος ἐξ Ἐρινύων δ. φρενῶν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 332 (Lyric poetry), compare 306.}}
**II** _passive_, **bound, captive,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 299, “Ph.” 608, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 226, “POxy.” 580 (2nd c.AD), etc.}}; **on leash,** [κύων] probably in {{Refs|Aen. Tact. 31.32}}; δ. φυγών, ={ἐκ δεσμῶν}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 792.}}

1199:
** δεσμός**, ὁ,
_plural_ δεσμά {{Refs 6th c.BC+|“hymnus ad Mercurium” 157, +others, 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 459, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.91}}, and so mostly in Trag., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 513, etc., and 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 9a; but δεσμοί 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 525, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 518, 634, usually in 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus, as “Lg.” 793b, +others}}: both forms in _Attic dialect_ Inscrr., δεσμοί {{Refs|“IG” 2.678 B 48, δεσμά [prev. work] 791.31}} the latter preferred by {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Thomas Epigrammaticus Mag. p.79R. (_sing._ δεσμόν “SIG” 246 ii 36 (Delph.)}}, _Attic dialect_ _accusative_ to {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus): (δέω A)}}:—**band, bond, anything for tying and fastening,** as **halter,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.507}}; **mooring-cable,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.100, etc.}}; **door-latch,** {{Refs|21.241}}; **yoke-strap,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.5.10}}: _metaphorically_, any **bond of union** or **connexion,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 31c, etc.}}; of the vowels, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Sph.” 253a}}; δεσμοὶ πολιτείας, of the laws, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Lg.” 793b}}; εἰς τὰ δεσμοῦ for **binding material,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|PTeb. 120.70 (1st c.BC).}}
**2** in _plural_ (never δεσμά in this sense), **bonds, chains,** ἐκ δεσμῶν λυθῆναι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 509, 770}}; πρὶν ἂν ἐξ ἀγρίων δ. χαλάσῃ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 177; ἐν δεσμοῖσι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 63; εἰς δεσμοὺς ἄγειν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 518; δεσμοῖς 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.82}}; ὁ ἐπὶ τῶν δ., ={δεσμοφύλαξ}, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tox.” 29}}: in _singular_, collectively, **bonds, imprisonment,** δ. ἀχλυόεις epigram cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.77 (= “IG” 12.394); οὐδὲν ἄξιον δεσμοῦ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.145; ἐν. δ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 958; ἐν δημοσίῳ δ. δεθείς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 864e; ἔδησεν ἑαυτὸν τιμησάμενος δεσμοῦ 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 6.21}}: _metaphorically_ of moral **bondage,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “de Abstinentia - Porphyrii Opuscula” 1.38, +others}}
**3** **ligature,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 495b13, +others}}
**4** δ. ἄρθρου in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 37 is explained by 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus}} (at [prev. work]) as **ankylosis.**
**5** **spell, charm,** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de Mysteriis” 3.27.}}
**II** ={δέσμη}, Papyrus in {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“Philol.” 80.341, 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 2.135, 12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 862.27; ἀσπαράγου δ. “BGU” 1120.14 (1st c.BC)}}; δ. ἀργυρίου {{LXX|LXX.Gen.42.27; compare δέσις 111.}}
